A perfect crystal never exists, since crystal growth and postsynthetic treatment always produces defects and damaged regions. Due to the tension and shear forces produced by component changes, the fragile single crystal tends to increase mosaicity and even crack into smaller pieces after guest desorption–adsorption and other post-synthetic treatments. Such phenomena may become more serious for many flexible PCPs exhibiting very significant structural changes. Therefore, single-crystal to singlecrystal (SCSC) transformation is rarely observed and is of particular interest and importance.19–23 In principle, a smaller specimen and slower change are beneficial to maintain the single-crystallinity of the sample. Fortunately, commercial single-crystal X-ray diffractometers are equipped with increasingly sensitive detectors and high-flux X-ray sources, as well as wide-operating-range temperature-controlling devices for in situ activation and measurement. Actually, SCXRD characterization of PCPs during/after the change of guest-loading and under other chemical/physical stimuli has served as a fundamental approach for studying the interesting properties of these materials.
While several excellent review articles discussing the significant framework flexibility, drastic SCSC structural transformation, and/ or solid-state reactivity of porous and nonporous coordination polymers have been published in recent years,19–27 this review focuses mainly on the role and challenge of SCXRD for studying the structural transformations of PCPs, with representative and recent examples. Based on the origins/mechanisms, i.e. change of solvent guest, change of gaseous guest, post-synthetic modification and physical stimuli, we categorize the structural transformations into four types. Structural transformations of PCPs are commonly induced by the changes of the species and/or loading amounts of guests, in which the chemical compositions of the PCP crystals are altered without chemical reaction on the host frameworks. Since the magnitudes of structural transformations and difficulties of structural characterizations are significantly different between solvent and gaseous guest molecules, they are discussed in two sections. In contrast, PCP crystals can be post-synthetically modified by chemical reactions in terms of both covalent and/or coordination bonding, in which not only crystals but also the host frameworks change their chemical compositions and structures. Finally, structural transformations of PCPs can be purely induced by physical stimuli such as temperature, in which the chemical compositions of the crystals are unchanged.
## 2. Structural transformation induced by the change of solvent guest
SCSC transformations of PCPs are mostly induced by desorption, adsorption or exchange of solvent molecules.28,29 An open framework is usually not the most thermodynamically favoured one among all reaction products.30 Templates, generally solvent molecules used in the synthesis, are included in the as-synthesized PCP crystals to stabilize the open framework structures. When the guest molecules are evacuated from the as-synthesized crystal, the coordination network may collapse irreversibly to form a nonporous structure, remain unchanged, or transform to a new structure that can reversibly adsorb/desorb some kinds of guest species. Only for the latter two cases, the crystals can be categorized as genuine PCPs.
Because of the relatively high boiling points and large molecular sizes, solvent/template molecules interact strongly with the host framework. Some solvent molecules can even coordinate with the metal ions to become a part of the host framework. The changes of such strong host–guest interactions, especially removal of the coordinated solvent molecules, could have a great possibility to induce a significant structural transformation. When the structural change is large enough, it can be easily noticed during routine PXRD characterizations. In some cases, reliable unit-cell volume and symmetry of the new phase can be obtained from high-quality PXRD patterns. Nevertheless, a precise crystal structure of the new phase is more useful for understanding its properties, which encourages many efforts to achieve a SCSC process to enable SCXRD measurement of the activated crystal. Depending on the role of solvent molecule and the degree of structural alternation, we categorize representative examples into the following four types.
## 2.1 Topology alternation induced by the change of coordinated solvent molecule
Metal ions and ligand donor atoms generally have a finite number of preferred coordination numbers and geometries. Except a few cases such as the elongated octahedral and squareplanar $\mathrm{{Cu}(\Omega_{\mathrm{{II}}})}$ ions, the maximum coordination number and corresponding geometry of a metal ion is much more stable than the lower ones. When a donor atom leaves the metal ion, an outer sphere donor would try to approach the vacant coordination site to maintain the original coordination number and geometry. In the restricted crystalline networks, such a ligand replacement process is much more difficult than in the solution states, but still frequently observed for coordination frameworks.31–37 To compensate for the removal of coordinated solvent molecule, the incoming ligand may come from another coordination network in the same crystal, which generates a new framework connectivity. Usually, this leads to an increase of the network dimensionality or complexity.
Kitagawa et al. described a series of reversible topochemical transformations between 2D and 3D coordination polymers induced by removal/addition of coordinated solvent molecules.37–39 For example, heating a single crystal of a 2D framework $\mathrm{\small{[Ln(tci)(H_{2}O)_{2}]{\cdot2H_{2}O}}}$ $\mathbf{\mathrm{(Ln~=~Ce}}$ or Pr, $\mathbf{H}_{3}\mathbf{tci}=$ tris(2-carboxyethyl)isocyanuric acid) at $80~^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ can remove all $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ molecules to give a 3D framework $\left[\mathrm{Ln}(\mathrm{tci})\right]$ (Fig. 1).37 The vacant sites produced by removal of two coordinated $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ molecules were occupied by two carboxylate oxygen atoms from another layer. The dehydrated product can adsorb $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ molecules to transform back to the as-synthesized compound, but it is inert to gases and organic solvents. The flexible ligand and lanthanide metal ions with high coordination numbers are believed to provide structural freedom to facilitate such a significant structural transformation.
The removal/addition of coordinated solvent molecules may change the interpenetration number of PCPs. For example, Kim et al. showed that heating a 3D PCP $[\mathrm{Zn}_{7}\mathrm{O}_{2}(\mathrm{nbd})_{5}(\mathrm{DMF})_{2}]$ (MOF-123; $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{b}\mathbf{d}\quad=\quad2$ -nitrobenzene-1,4-dicarboxylic acid; $\mathbf{DMF}=N,N\mathrm{.}$ -dimethylformamide) with non-interpenetrated pcu topology can remove the coordinated DMF to form a nonporous crystal $[\mathrm{Zn}_{7}\mathrm{O}_{2}(\mathrm{nbd})_{5}]$ (MOF-246) with 2-fold interpenetrated pcu topology (Fig. 2).40 The colour, morphology and size of the crystal change obviously (contracted by ca. $50\%$ in thickness) in such a transformation. The reversed transformation can be furnished by soaking the crystal MOF-246 in DMF at $180~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . A possible SCSC transformation mechanism was proposed. After removal of the coordinating DMF molecules, the $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}_{7}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ coordination sphere is distorted, which forces half of the $\mathrm{nbd}^{2-}$ linkers to leave the $\mathbf{Zn}_{7}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ units, forming layered fragments. Subsequently, each layer slides into the pore of the neighbouring layer to reduce the empty space. Finally, each layer connects to two next nearest neighbours by the unsaturated $\mathbf{Zn}_{7}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ units and $\mathrm{nbd}^{2-}$ ligands, generating the interpenetrated structure.
Besides removal/addition of coordinated solvent molecules, the network topologies of the host frameworks can be also altered by solvent exchange at the coordination sites. Lin et al. reported a 2D PCP $\mathrm{\small{[Zn_{2}(m t c)(D M F)_{4}]cdot2D M F{\cdot}4H_{2}O}}$ ( $\mathrm{\:\:\cdot\:}_{\mathrm{H_{4}m t c}}=$ methanetetra(biphenyl- $p$ -carboxylic acid)) with 4-fold interpenetrated hinged-layer structure and a small BET surface area of $177~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ .41 Soaking the as-synthesized crystal in $\mathbf{CH}_{2}\mathbf{Cl}_{2}$ at room temperature for $^{8\mathrm{~h~}}$ gave a 3D structure $[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{mtc})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}].$ G $\mathbf{\bar{G}}=\mathbf{\bar{\Lambda}}$ uncertain guest molecules) with 4-fold interpenetrated dia topology and a large BET surface area of $1170\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1}$ (Fig. 3). This example is unique as the 2D to 3D structural transformation increases the porosity and sorption capacity. In the 2D phase, each $\mathrm{Zn(n)}$ ion is chelated by two carboxylate groups from two bridging $\mathrm{mtc}^{4-}$ ligands and two terminal DMF molecules to form a mononuclear connection. The complete exchange of four coordinated DMF molecules by two water molecules reduces the distance between two mononuclear $\mathrm{Zn(n)}$ centres, which are bridged by three $\upmu$ -carboxylate ends into a dinuclear 4-connected node in the 3D phase. To prove the SCSC transformation mechanism in the solvent immersion process, crystals of $\mathrm{\small{[Zn_{2}(m t c)(D M F)_{4}]cdot2D M F{\cdot}4H_{2}O}}$ and a $\operatorname{Co}(\operatorname{II})$ analogue were soaked in close proximity in $\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ at room temperature. After more than $^\textrm{\scriptsize3h}$ , the morphologies of the crystals remained unchanged, and the 2D to 3D structural transformation was complete. Inductively coupled plasmamass spectrometry (ICP-MS) analysis of crystals showed that the crystal of $\mathrm{\big[Zn_{2}(m t c)(H_{2}O)_{2}\big]{\cdot}G}$ did not contain Co and the $\operatorname{Co}(\operatorname{II})$ analogue did not contain Zn.
The bridging ligand can be also replaced by solvent molecules to change its network connectivity. Bu et al. reported that when a self-penetrating (3,8)-connected network $\mathrm{\small{[Co_{1.5}(t i p b)(S O_{4})(b d c)_{0.5}]}}$ 1.75DMF $(\mathrm{tipb}=1,3,5\mathrm{-tris}(p$ -imidazolylphenyl)benzene, $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{b}\mathbf{d}\mathbf{c}=$ 1,4-benzenediarboxylic acid) was exposed to air, the blue crystals gradually turned red without losing single-crystallinity. SCXRD showed that the red crystal possessed a formula of $\big[\mathrm{Co}_{1.5}(\mathrm{tipb})(\mathrm{SO}_{4})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{3.6}\big](\mathrm{bdc})_{0.5}\cdot\mathrm{G},$ in which the original bdc2 linker left the coordination network, and became a highly disordered counter anion as confirmed by nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) and high performance liquid chromatography (HPLC). The $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ molecule became a terminal ligand, and the $\operatorname{Co}(\operatorname{II})$ coordination geometry also changed from tetrahedral to octahedral. Consequently, the host coordination framework was divided into two (3,6)-connected sit networks (Fig. 4).42 On the other hand, activation of the as-synthesized crystal by sequential methanol exchange and heating gave isostructural $\left[\mathrm{Co_{1.5}(t i p b)(S O_{4})(b d c)_{0.5}}\right]$ with poor crystallinity.
## 2.2 Framework distortion induced by the change of coordinated solvent molecule
Long-distance ligand replacement can not occur in many PCPs. For rigid PCPs similar to zeolites, coordinatively unsaturated or open metal sites can be obtained by removal of terminal coordinated solvent molecules. Obviously, open metal sites are energetically unstable, which can be used to strongly bind guests of interest. Nevertheless, to reach the local energy minimum, flexible PCPs tend to distort the whole network and local coordination geometry, or utilize short-distance donors to make the open metal site less open. Compared with examples showing topology alternations, framework distortions induced by the change of coordinated solvent molecules are usually small in extent.43,44
Besides distortion of the whole coordination network, the change of coordinated solvent molecules may induce very significant structural transformations around the coordination center, which can be readily visualized by SCXRD rather than PXRD. For example, Rosseinsky et al. reported an interesting reversible ligand substitution SCSC transformation between pink $[\mathrm{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{bpy)}_{3}(\mathrm{SO}_{4})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}]$ bpy $\mathrm{\bf{C}H_{3}O H}$ $(\mathsf{b p y}=4,4^{\prime}$ -bipyridine) and purple $[\mathrm{Co_{2}(b p y)_{3}(S O_{4})_{2}(b p y)(C H_{3}O H)}]$ . SCXRD showed that the vacant coordination site generated by removal of the coordinated $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ molecule was occupied by the lattice MeOH and bpy guests, in which MeOH and bpy molecules moved by 4.4 and $2.8\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , respectively (Fig. 5). Meanwhile, due to the increased steric requirement of bpy, the coordination network was also distorted slightly, leading to a small change of crystal volume and shape.
In many cases, distortions of the host framework and/or the crystal lattice may be insignificant and even seemingly ignorable.46–49 For example, a porous magnetic metal carboxylate framework $\mathrm{\small{[KCo_{7}(O H)_{3}(i p a)_{6}(H_{2}O)_{4}]{\cdot}12H_{2}O}}$ (MCF-17; $\mathrm{\bf{H}}_{2}\mathrm{\ipa\=\}$ isophthalic acid) retained single-crystallinity but changed colour from red to purple after complete removal of both lattice and coordinated $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ molecules.50 Although the PXRD patterns of the red and purple crystals are very similar, SCXRD analysis of the purple crystal of $\mathrm{[KCo_{7}(O H)_{3}(i p a)_{6}]}$ indicated that their coordination geometries are very different. Most remarkably, a Co(II) ion changed its coordination geometry from an octahedron to a configuration between a tetrahedron and distorted trigonal-bipyramid (Fig. 6). When the dehydrated crystal was exposed to air for a few days, the purple crystals returned to red without loss of single-crystallinity. Distortion of the coordination geometry of $\operatorname{Co}(\operatorname{II})$ ions and change of coordinated and lattice $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ molecules significantly switched the magnetic property between spin-canting for the hydrated phase and antiferromagnetism for the dehydrated phase.
In another example, there was no visual indication on the colour or PXRD pattern about structural transformation.
The single crystal of as-synthesized $\mathrm{\small{[Gd(btc)(H_{2}O)]cdot D M F{\cdot}0.5H_{2}O}}$ $\left(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{btc}\right.=1,3,5$ -benzenetricarboxylic acid) always cracked and became opaque by direct heating for guest removal.51 To facilitate crystal activation, the crystals were exchanged by $\mathbf{CH}_{2}\mathbf{Cl}_{2}$ . The $\mathbf{CH}_{2}\mathbf{Cl}_{2}$ -included crystal became severely and irregularly distorted to form multiple domains, impeding determination of its single-crystal structure. However, highquality SCXRD data was measured for guest-free $\left[\mathrm{Gd}(\mathrm{btc})\right]$ obtained by heating the $\mathbf{CH}_{2}\mathbf{Cl}_{2}$ -exchanged crystal at $200~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ , which revealed that the $\operatorname{Gd}(\operatorname{III})$ ions were exposed on the channel surface with slightly altered coordination geometry. $\left[\mathrm{Gd}(\mathrm{btc})\right]$ showed notable selectivity for oxygen-containing solvent molecules. Immersing [Gd(btc)] in EtOH and EtOH–MeCN mixture gave single crystals of $[\mathrm{Gd}(\mathrm{btc})(\mathrm{EtOH})]$ EtOH and $\mathrm{[Gd(btc)(EtOH)]{\cdot}M e C N}$ , respectively. Interestingly, $\mathrm{[Gd(btc)(EtOH)]}$ EtOH possessed a polar structure (space group $\scriptstyle P4_{1_{j}} $ ), while the guest-free and other solvent-loaded phases were non-polar (space group $P4_{1}22_{.}$ ). Although the unit-cell parameters and PXRD patterns of $\left[\mathbf{G}\mathbf{d}(\mathbf{b}\mathbf{tc})(\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O})\right]$ DMF, $\mathrm{[Gd(btc)(EtOH)]{\cdot}M e C N}$ and $\mathrm{[Gd(btc)(EtOH)]{\cdot}E t O H}$ are very similar, their host frameworks distort very differently (Fig. 7). More importantly, the coordinated EtOH and lattice MeCN molecules could be clearly distinguished in $\mathrm{[Gd(btc)(EtOH)]{\cdot}M e C N}$ , demonstrating the oxophilicity of open $\operatorname{Gd}(\operatorname{m})$ centres.
## 2.3 Topology transformation induced by the change of lattice solvent molecules
Lattice solvent molecules interact with the host framework with hydrogen bonding, $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking and other weak interactions. Removal/adsorption of lattice solvent molecules hardly changes the coordination network topology or connectivity. Available examples,52,53 especially of SCSC type, are still very scarce.
We observed an example of reversible SCSC alteration of interpenetration number in $\mathrm{\Gamma[Ag_{6}C l(a t z)_{4}]O H{\cdot}}n H_{2}O$ (Hatz $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ 3-amino-1,2,4-triazole) induced by removal/addition of lattice water molecules.54 The as-synthesized compound $\mathrm{\small{[Ag_{6}C l(a t z)_{4}]O H{\cdot}6H_{2}O}}$ possessed a cationic framework consisting of two-coordinated Ag(I) ions and three-coordinated triazolate ligands, which interconnected to form 5-fold interpenetrated dia-f networks, retaining large 1D channels (diameter $d\approx8.5\mathring\mathrm{A}$ ) occupied by counter anions and guest water molecules. Conventional activation led to a shrunk phase with cracked crystals. The single-crystal structure of the shrunk phase was obtained as $\mathrm{\small{[Ag_{6}C l(a t z)_{4}]O H}}.$ $2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ by slow and partial dehydration, which revealed not only drastic shrinkage and distortion of the framework and 1D channels ( $\mathbf{4.3}\mathrm{~\overset{\circ}{A}~}\times\mathbf{1}0.4\mathrm{~\overset{\circ}{A}~}$ , but also a 6-fold interpenetration number (Fig. 8). The Ag(I) ions near the pore surface of $\mathrm{\small{[Ag_{6}C l(a t z)_{4}]O H{\cdot}6H_{2}O}}$ are linearly coordinated, which changed to an obviously bent coordination configuration and formed weak contacts with the remaining counter anions and/or lattice $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ molecules. Based on these structural characteristics, a possible structural transformation mechanism was deduced, in which the counter anion and/or lattice $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ coordinated with Ag(I) to form three-coordinated intermediates to facilitate the breakage of $\mathrm{\sf~Ag(\mathrm{\mathrm{I})}}$ –triazolate bonds, and the interpenetration number can be changed by a shift of adjacent connections.
On the other hand, Du et al. showed that removing lattice solvent molecules by different routes can lead to completely different types of SCSC transformations.55 The as-synthesized 3D PCP $\mathrm{[Cu(iba)_{2}]\cdot2H_{2}O}$ (Hiba $\mathit{\Delta}=\mathit{\Delta}4$ -( $1H\cdot$ -imidazol-1-yl)benzoic acid) with a 3-fold interpenetrated lvt topology can be dehydrated to form two isomeric guest-free phases $[\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{ib}\mathrm{a})_{2}]$ either retaining the original topology or transforming to a $\mathrm{2D\kgm}$ network (Fig. 9). The former case occurred when the assynthesized crystal was heated at $160~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ , while the latter case was fulfilled by exposing the as-synthesized crystal in air at room temperature for about two months. The guest-free lvt framework can be rehydrated to the as-synthesized state, but the lvt to kgm structural transformation is irreversible.
In some cases, alteration of the lattice solvent molecules can significantly change the coordination geometry and local connectivity of the building blocks. Kitagawa et al. showed that the dinuclear paddle-wheel unit in $[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{bdc})_{2}(\mathrm{bpb})]{\cdot}2.5\mathrm{DMF}{\cdot}0.5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $(\mathrm{bpb}=2,3$ -difluoro-1,4-bis(4-pyridyl)benzene) can undergo reversible metal–ligand bond breaking/formation (Fig. 10).56 Heating the as-synthesized crystals at $115~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under vacuum gave guestfree $[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{bdc})_{2}(\mathrm{bpb})]$ . SCXRD showed that the coordination environment of the $\mathrm{Zn(n)}$ centres changed from square pyramidal to tetrahedral, while one of the two carboxylate groups changed from bidentate to monodentate with the $Z\mathrm{n-O}$ distances being changed from 2.043(5) to 5.164(14) $\mathring\mathbf{A}.$ As a consequence, the conventional paddle-wheel cluster transformed to an open configuration, although the overall network topology was unchanged. Drastic shrinkage of the unit-cell volume (2070 to 1492 $\mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{3}$ , $28\%$ ) and length of the $c$ -axis (18.52 to 14.99 Å, $19\%$ ), as well as the solvent accessible volume $(40.4\%$ to $6.5\%$ ) were also observed due to the desolvation, which should be the main force for changing coordination topology of the paddle-wheel cluster. By controlled desolvation, two intermediate structures were also obtained and characterized by SCXRD. Interestingly, these transformations can be reversed by Ar, $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ and $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ , as demonstrated by PXRD, Raman spectroscopy and gas sorption isotherms.
## 2.4 Framework distortion induced by the change of lattice solvent molecules
In most flexible PCPs, adsorption–desorption of lattice solvent molecules only distorts the host framework. Without cleavage/ formation of coordination bonds, the structural transformation can still be very significant, since framework flexibility is generated by not only the versatile coordination geometries of metal ions but also by rotatable metal–ligand bonds, soft organic ligands and supramolecular packing.
Depending on the topology of the coordination network and flexibility mode of the building blocks, PCPs can show very different types and magnitudes of framework distortion.57–59 For example, F´erey et al. developed a highly breathable family of frameworks $\big[\mathbf{M}_{3}\big({\upmu}_{3}{\cdot}\mathbf{O}\big)(\mathrm{ldc})_{3}(\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O})_{2}(\mathbf{X})\big]{\cdot}\mathbf{C}$ (MIL-88s, ${\bf M}={\bf F}{\bf e}$ or Cr, $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{l}\mathbf{dc}=$ linear dicarboxylic acid, $\mathbf{X}^{-}=\mathbf{\partial}$ coordinated anion) with a 6-connected acs network (hexagonal symmetry) based on trigonal-prismatic nodes and linear dicarboxylate linkers (Fig. 11), in which the framework distortion degrees can be controlled by the length and the substituent group of the linear dicarboxylate, as well as the type of lattice solvent molecules.60–62
These structures expand by shortening the $c$ -axis and simultaneously expanding the $a b$ -plane. The largest volumetric and axial breathing amplitudes, $237\%$ and $100\%$ , respectively, were observed in the crystals constructed by $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -biphenyldicarboxylate. Because large crystals could not be obtained, these compounds and transformations were mostly studied by PXRD.
Subtle modification of the network connectivity may drastically alter the framework distortion mode. Recently, we reported a flexible PCP showing similar network topology but reversed framework breathing direction with MIL-88s. A (3,9)-connected xmz (trigonal symmetry)63 framework $\left[\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\upmu_{3}\mathbf{-OH})(\mathrm{dcb})_{3}\right]$ (MCF-18, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{d}\mathrm{c}\mathrm{b}=2,6{\cdot}\mathrm{d}\mathrm{i}{\cdot}p\cdot$ carboxyphenyl- $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine) was constructed by trigonal pyridyldicarboxylate ligands and tricapped trigonalprismatic $\mathrm{Ni_{3}(\mu_{3}{-}O H)(R C O O)_{6}(R p y)_{3}}$ $\mathbf{\bar{RCOO}}^{-}=$ carboxylate group; $\mathrm{\bfRpy}\mathrm{~=~}$ pyridyl group) clusters (Fig. 11).64 PXRD showed that MCF-18 can drastically swell by $70–105\%$ in volume and $75\mathrm{-}121\%$ in length upon inclusion of different solvent guests. Because of the restriction on the equatorial sites by coordination of pyridyl ends, MCF-18 distorted almost only at the $c$ -axis, giving rise to exceptionally large axial breathing amplitude. The breathing amplitudes of MCF-18 for different solvents followed a sequence similar for MIL-88s. Although single crystals of MCF-18 tended to crack during desolvation and solvent exchange, single-crystal structures were successfully measured for MCF-18, MCF-18 MeOH, MCF-18 DMF and MCF-18 pyridine, demonstrating that bending of the metal–carboxylate junction and conformational change of the organic ligand were mainly responsible for the breathing behaviour. The MeOH lattice molecules were highly disordered and could not be modeled, while the DMF guest molecules were relatively ordered and modeled with some geometric restraints. Interestingly, other known coordination polymers with the xmz topology (including nonporous and highly porous structures with similar porosity as MCF-18) did not show solvent-induced framework breathing.63,65 Detailed comparison showed that the shape of the organic ligand in MCF-18 restricted the $\mathrm{Ni_{3}(\mu_{3}{-}O/\mu_{3}{-}O H)(R C O O)_{6}(L^{T})_{3}}$ ( $\mathbf{\bar{L}^{T}=}$ terminal ligand) cluster in a geometry that is bendable and requires a reversed bending direction compared with those observed in MIL-88s (Fig. 11).
Pillared-rod structures constructed by inorganic chains and ditopic organic ligands are a typical type of PCPs showing anisotropic framework breathing.66,67 For example, Long et al. reported a PCP $\begin{array}{r}{[{\bf C u(b d t)}({\bf{\upmu}}{\bf{\cdot D M F}})]{\bf{\cdot}}\bullet\qquad}\end{array}$ G $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{bdt}\ =\ 1,4\right.$ -benzenedi(1H-1,2,3-triazole)) showing large fence-like framework breathing controlled by the loading of lattice solvent molecules.68 In this structure, infinite $\mathbf{Cu(\upmu-DMF)(Rtz)}_{2}$ $\mathbf{\bar{R}}\mathbf{t}\mathbf{z}^{-}=$ triazolate group) rods were pillared by the linear ligands to form a 3D framework, retaining 1D channels running along the rods. Upon exposure to ambient air, the framework released lattice solvent molecules and underwent two sequential SCSC transformations, changing the unit-cell volume from $2186\mathrm{~}\mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{3}$ to $1937\ \mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{3}$ and finally to $1567\ \mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{3}$ , but there was almost no change in the rod direction (Fig. 12). Detailed analyses of single-crystal structures showed that the geometry changes associated with flexing of the bridging ligand was the main reason for the shrinkage of the framework. PXRD showed that the breathing was reversible, but the intermediate state could not be captured.
Huang et al. reported a similar multi-step SCSC breathing example in $\mathrm{\small{[Zn_{3}(\mu_{3}-O H)_{2}(b t a c)_{2}]{\cdot D M F{\cdot}}4H_{2}O}}$ $\mathbf{\tilde{H}}_{2}\mathbf{b}\mathbf{tac}=$ benzotriazole-5-carboxylic acid) consisting of infinite zigzag-ladder rod-shape $\mathrm{Zn}_{3}(\upmu_{3}\mathbf{-OH})_{2}(\mathbf{RCOO})_{2}(\mathbf{Rtz})_{2}$ chains.69 Interestingly, the $\operatorname{Co}(\operatorname{II})$ analogue of this compound is rigid.70 A converse phenomena was observed in isostructural $[\mathrm{Zn(bdp)}]$ and $\mathrm{[co(bdp)]}$ $(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{bdp}~=~1,4$ -benzenedipyrazole) by Long et al. $(\mathrm{Co}\mathrm{(n)}$ compound possessed much higher flexibility than the $\mathrm{Zn(n)}$ analogue),71,72 which can be attributed to the stereochemical rigidity of the $\mathrm{ZnN_{4}}$ tetrahedron.73 These results demonstrate that besides the framework connectivity, local coordination structure and type of lattice solvent, metal ion and amount of lattice solvent are also important parameters in controlling the framework flexibility/rigidity and breathing amplitude.
With isotropic network topologies, the framework distortion manner could occur more freely because the anisotropic breathing modes of building blocks are averaged. We have observed different types of framework distortions in a hydrophobic porous metal azolate framework $\mathrm{[Cu(detz)]}$ (MAF-2, Hdetz $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}_{3,5}$ -diethyltriazole), which can shrink, expand and distort to respond the inclusion of different lattice solvent molecules (Fig. 13).74 MAF-2 possesses an nbo-type coordination framework and a bcu-type channel system (the maximum symmetries of nbo and bcu topologies are cubic). Each aperture between adjacent nbo cages are surrounded by six ethyl groups, resulting in a very small effective size $(d\approx1.5\mathring\mathrm{A})$ in the crystal structure. Room-temperature solvent vapour sorption measurements showed that MAF-2 can adsorb stoichiometric and large amounts of organic solvents MeOH, EtOH, MeCN and $\mathrm{C_{6}H_{6}}$ , but completely exclude $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ and oversized $\mathbf{C}_{6}\mathbf{H}_{12}$ even close to the saturation vapour pressures. Since the crystallographic aperture size is obviously smaller than the sizes of the adsorbed molecules, the ethyl-blocked apertures have to open, by conformational change of the ethyl groups and even distortion of the $\mathrm{{Cu}(\mathrm{{I})}}$ –triazolate backbone, for guest diffusion. In principle, conformational change of ethyl groups is sufficient for passing MeOH, EtOH, MeCN and smaller guests, and backbone distortion is necessary for benzene but may not be enough for larger guests. SCSC transformations were accomplished between the guest-free phase and the MeOH-, EtOH- and MeCN-included phases, but adsorption of benzene destroyed the singlecrystallinity of MAF-2, which can be explained by the greater damage induced by backbone distortion.
Due to the rotatable coordination bonds, the coordination framework of MAF-2 can distort significantly to adopt different crystal symmetries (Fig. 13). The MeOH- and EtOH-included, and guest-free phases possess the trigonal space group $R\bar{3}$ . Inside each nbo cage, six alcohol molecules form a chair-like supramolecular hexamer sustained by $\mathbf{O}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{H}\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{O}$ hydrogen bonds $\left(\mathbf{O}^{.}\cdot\cdot\mathbf{O}\ 2.67–2.69\ \mathring\mathrm{A}\right)$ . At the same temperature of $123\mathrm{~K~}$ , the unit-cell volume of the guest-free phase $(4766\ \mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{3})$ is $1.8\%$ larger than the MeOH-included phase $(4682\ \mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{3})$ but $1.1\%$ smaller than the EtOH-included one (4819 $\mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{3\cdot}$ indicating that MeOH hexamer is smaller than the cavity and attracts the host framework leading to shrinkage, while the EtOH hexamer is larger than the cavity and expands the framework by steric repulsion. The MeCN-included phase possesses the triclinic $P\bar{1}$ space group, in which the MeCN molecules aggregate with multiple ${\mathrm{C-H}}\cdot\cdot\mathbf{N}$ interactions $\left(\mathbf{C}\cdot\cdot\cdot\mathbf{N}3.52\mathring\mathrm{A}\right)$ to form a unique hexamer with asymmetrically flattened octahedral geometry. Interestingly, the solvent molecules interact with the pore surface with their alkyl groups rather than the more polar hydroxyl or nitrile groups, demonstrating the hydrophobic nature of MAF-2.
Similar to other examples (Fig. 7), SCXRD is necessary for visualizing the small yet important structural transformations occurring for MAF-2. On the other hand, the host framework structure of the benzene-included phase of MAF-2 was deduced from its PXRD pattern, as it is similar to the cubic phase of MAF-2 synthesized by in situ oxidative cycloaddition of propionitrile and ammonia in the presence of Cu(II).54 According to the benzene sorption isotherm, each nbo cage accommodate three benzene molecules, whose location is obviously not compatible with the cubic symmetry.
Besides expansion, shrinkage and distortion of a single coordination network, interframework sliding between multiple interpenetrated or packed coordination networks can also distort the PCP crystal.29,75–79 For example, we reported that a PCP $\mathrm{[Fe(pydc)(bpy)]{\cdot}H_{2}O}$ $\mathrm{\bf~\tilde{H}_{2}p y d c}=$ pyridine-2,5-dicarboxylic acid, bpy $\mathit{\Theta}=4,4^{\prime}$ -bipyridine) consisting of 2D thick layers can exhibit SCSC sliding of the layers triggered by guest exchange, dehydration and readsorption (Fig. 14).79 Activating the assynthesized crystal $\mathrm{[Fe(pydc)(bpy)]{\cdot}H_{2}O}$ under a $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ atmosphere at $160~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ yielded anhydrous $[\mathrm{Fe(pydc)(bpy)}]$ with an $8.2\%$ smaller unit-cell volume. Comparison of their single-crystal structures showed that the 2D layers remain almost unchanged, but their packing mode varied from slightly offset to highly canted stacking.
More commonly, interframework sliding and framework distortion occur simultaneously.80 For example, Kitagawa et al. reported a flexible PCP $\mathrm{[Zn_{2}(b d c)_{2}(d p n d i)]{\cdot}4D M F}$ (dpndi $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ $N,N^{\prime}$ -di(4-pyridyl)-1,4,5,8-naphthalenediimide) with 2-fold interpenetrated jungle-gym structure, which showed multimode structural transformation and luminescence responses for aromatic guests.77 Depending on the type and amount of solvent molecules, the 2-fold interpenetrated coordination networks can change interframework and host–guest interactions significantly by adopting different degrees of network distortion and different mutual positions between the two coordination networks (Fig. 15).
Schro¨der et al. reported a flexible PCP $\big(\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\big)_{1.75}[\mathrm{In(bptc)}]_{1.75}$  12DMF $\boldsymbol{10\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ (NOTT-202, $\mathbf{H_{4}b p t c}=$ biphenyl- $^{3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}}$ -tetra(phenyl4-carboxylic acid)) with 2-fold interpenetrated 3D binodal 4-connected networks.81 Upon guest removal, the crystal exhibited an expansion in the unit cell by $11\%$ and solvent-accessible volume by $10\%$ due to framework distortion including an increase of $\bf{I n}\mathrm{-}\bf{O}$ bond length from 2.355(7) to 2.774(3) Å. More interestingly, sliding between the two interpenetrated increased the channel size from $5.0\times5.0$ to $9.0\times9.0\stackrel{\circ}{\mathbf{A}}^{2}$ (Fig. 16).
In special cases, interframework sliding and framework distortion occur in very complicated manners. For example, Kitagawa et al. reported a highly symmetric porous framework $\big[\mathrm{Ag}_{2}\big(\mathrm{bpz}\big)\big]$ $({\bf H}_{2}{\bf b p z}\ =\ 3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}$ -tetrametyl- $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bipyrazole) with 4-fold interpenetrated srs topology crystallizing in the cubic space group $I\bar{4}3d$ $(Z=48)$ ).82 In this structure, three Ag(I) ions were coordinated by three pyrazolate moieties to form the classic triangular $\mathrm{\Ag_{3}(R p z)_{3}}$ $\left({\bf R}{\bf p}{\bf z}^{-}\ =\ \right.$ pyrazolate group) units, which were exposed on the pore surface and suitable to interact with planar aromatic molecules in the face-to-face fashion. Since the two pyrazolate rings in the $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{bpz}^{2-}}$ ligand can rotate around their joined $\scriptstyle\mathbf{C-C}$ single bond, the coordination network was highly distortable. Upon saturated adsorption of benzene $\mathrm{(C_{6}H_{6})}$ , toluene $\mathrm{{(C_{7}H_{8})}}$ and mesitylene $\mathbf{\left(C_{9}H_{12}\right)}$ in the lattice, the crystal transformed to tetragonal (space group $I4_{1}/a$ , $Z=16$ ) with quintuple unit-cell volumes, meaning that their structural complexities were 15 times that of the guest-free phase.
The aromatic molecules were sandwiched by pairs of $\mathsf{A g}_{3}(\mathsf{p z})_{3}$ units as monomers and different types of dimers and trimers in the very complicated guest-included phases $\mathrm{\small{[Ag_{30}(b p z)_{15}]cdot10C_{6}H_{6}}},$ $[\mathrm{Ag}_{30}(\mathrm{bpz)_{15}]{\cdot}9\mathrm{C}_{7}\mathrm{H}_{8}}$ and $\left[\mathrm{Ag}_{30}(\mathrm{bpz)_{15}}\right]\cdot8\mathrm{C}_{9}\mathrm{H}_{12}$ . Since the whole crystal showed very small expansion, the simultaneous shrinkage and expansion of pores in different degrees can be regarded as a result of complicated local framework distortion and interframework sliding. Detailed analyses of these crystal structures showed that the $\mathsf{b p z}^{2-}$ adopt a variety of conformations (Fig. 17). More interestingly, with partial loading, the single-crystal specimen can reversibly transform to other single-crystalline phases (one of which has been solved by SCXRD although the amount and locations of disordered solvent molecules could not be determined) and even amorphous phase, which further illustrate its robustness and flexibility.83
Actually, many flexible PCPs showed reversible crystal-toamorphous,84 and even continuous structural transformations from one single crystal to amorphous, and then to another single crystal. In these cases, the amorphous phases should exhibit non-periodical framework distortion rather than framework collapse. For example, Morris et al. studied the flexibility of $\mathrm{\small{[Cu_{2}(O H)(s i p)(H_{2}O)]\cdot2H_{2}O}}$ (Cu-sip-3; $\mathbf{H}_{3}\mathbf{sip}\ =\ \mathbf{5}$ -sulfoisophthalic acid) using in situ SCXRD at variable temperature.85 A series of diffraction data sets were collected for about every $15\mathrm{~K~}$ interval from 150 to $500~\mathrm{K},$ , and the occupancies of the three crystallographically independent water molecules were allowed to refine freely. Based on the change of occupancies, partial loss of guest water molecules were observed between 150 and $370\mathrm{~K~}$ , during which the host framework showed gradual distortion. Between 370 and $405\mathrm{~K~}$ corresponding to the removal of other $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ molecules, no discrete, sharp Bragg peak can be seen by SCXRD. Therefore, the authors utilized the pair distribution function (PDF) method, a total scattering technique that does not require crystalline order to be informative, to visualize the complicated local structural transformations.18 Above $405\mathrm{~K~}$ , the Bragg peaks return and the structure can be refined as the dehydrated phase $\mathrm{[Cu_{2}(O H)(s i p)]}$ demonstrating the crystal did not collapse but just lost long-range order at $370{-}405\mathrm{K}.$
Although the structural transformations of PCPs induced by the change of solvent guests are discussed above in four typical types, some PCPs can show two or more types of such structural transformations. For example, Shimizu et al. reported a porous metal sulfonate framework showing very interesting multi-step SCSC dehydration.86 The as-synthesized compound $\mathrm{\big[Ba_{3}\big(H_{2}O\big)_{6}(b t s\big)_{2}\big]}.$ $8\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ 1 $\mathbf{\hat{H}}_{3}\mathbf{b}\mathbf{t}\mathbf{s}=$ benzene-1,3,5-trisulfonic acid) can sequentially transform to $\mathrm{\big[Ba_{3}(H_{2}O)_{6}(b t s)_{2}]{\cdot}2H_{2}O}$ and $\mathrm{\big[Ba_{3}\big(H_{2}O\big)_{6}(b t s\big)_{2}\big]}$ by standing in air, and finally to $\big[\mathrm{Ba}_{3}(\mathrm{bts})_{2}\big]$ by heating, during which not only significant framework shrinkage, but also relocation of coordinated $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ molecules, alteration of coordination numbers, and rearrangement of the coordination connectivity were observed.
## 3. Structural transformation induced by the change of gaseous guest
Gases, especially permanent gases, have much lower boiling points and smaller molecular sizes than liquid/solvents. Due to the very weak interaction between gas molecules and host framework, gas-induced structural transformations are generally small in extent, but it can be still crucial for the adsorption mechanism. Since most in-house diffractometers are not convenient to measure PXRD for gas-loaded PCPs, gas-induced structural transformations are commonly suggested by abnormal gas adsorption isotherms. Nevertheless, without a gas-loaded crystal structure, the abnormal gas adsorption isotherm can be also explained by relocation and/or layer-by-layer adsorption of guest molecules. Although molecular dynamic calculation is emerging as a powerful tool for simulating the adsorption mechanism,87–90 measuring the guest-loaded crystal structure is still the most reliable and straightforward approach. Nevertheless, crystal structures of gas-loaded PCPs are still rarely reported. So far most examples are measured by synchrotron and neutron PXRD,91–95 probably because of the difficulty in preparing single-crystal samples, enthusiasm in hydrogen storage in recent years, and availability of advanced temperature/pressure controlling accessories.
For PCPs with different adsorption sites and/or gas-induced structural transformations, the sequential adsorption processes can be monitored by measuring crystal structures at different temperatures and/or gas pressures. For large-pore PCPs with weak gas affinity, this can be done by variabletemperature measurement with a crystal sealed with the targeted gas in a glass capillary, during which the temperature and pressure are simultaneously changed, but the gas amount in the capillary (including the crystal) is constant. Although the precise pressure is unknown at each measurement temperature, this simple method can give information somewhat similar to the gas sorption isotherm/isobar. For example, single-crystal structures of $[\mathrm{Zn}_{4}\mathrm{O}(\mathrm{bdc})_{3}]$ (MOF-5) sealed in glass capillary with either Ar or ${\bf N}_{2}$ illustrated eight symmetryindependent adsorption sites sequentially from 293 to $30~\mathrm{K}$ .96 For small-pore PCPs with strong gas affinity, excess gas in the capillary always leads to saturated adsorption even at high temperatures. To vary the gas loading precisely, the gas pressure must be controlled. For ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption, the crystal may be exposed in the temperature-controlling nitrogen flow to carry out constant-pressure (1 atm) and variable-temperature measurements, which reflect the isobars and similarly the isotherms. More commonly, the gas pressures are roughly controlled by sealing the crystal at different conditions, in which the exact gas pressure is unknown for the measurement. Precise control of gas pressure can be achieved by connecting the sample tube with a gas-dosing manifold, which has been widely utilized in PXRD,93,97–99 but is rarely used in SCXRD.100
To minimize the serious thermal motion and disorder of gas molecules, crystal structures of gas-loaded PCPs are usually measured at temperatures as low as possible at the saturated adsorption state. In general, even at the just-mentioned optimum states, determination/refinement of a gas-loaded crystal structure is still very difficult, in which restraints/constraints on molecular geometries and/or thermal parameters are usually necessary.101,102 Depending on the measurement method and condition, sample quality and nature, as well as the type of gas, the obtained crystal structures vary substantially in quality and can provide very different amounts of structural information. Compared with PXRD,92,103 the high-resolution of SCXRD is particularly beneficial for determination of gas-loaded crystal structures, in which the precise adsorption site, orientation and thermal motion of gas molecules, as well as the small structural alternation of the host framework can be obtained. In exceptional cases, usually for high boiling-point gases such as $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ , high-quality SCXRD data and strong adsorption/confinement can be obtained to reveal the thermal motion of gas molecules, even at room temperature.104,105 Some recent examples are discussed below.
Takamizawa et al. used SCXRD to study the adsorption behaviours of a variety of gases in flexible PCPs $[{\mathbf{M}}^{\mathrm{II}}{}_{2}(\mathrm{bza})_{4}-$ (Rpyz)] $(\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{C}\mathbf{u}$ , Rh; Hbza $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ benzoic acid; Rpyz $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ pyrazine derivatives) consisting of parallelly packed 1D chains and isolated cavities.106 For example, $[\mathrm{Rh_{2}(b z a)_{4}(p y z)}]$ showed a typical two-step $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ isotherm at $195\mathrm{~K~}$ and a high $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption enthalpy of $43\mathrm{~kJ~mol}^{-1}$ at zero coverage.107,108 A $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ saturated single-crystal structure was measured at $90~\mathrm{K}$ , which adopted the triclinic space group $P\bar{1}$ . On the other hand, the guest-free crystal was monoclinic ( $\dot{C}2/c$ at $90~\mathrm{K}$ and $C2/m$ and $298\mathrm{~K~}$ , $T_{\mathrm{c}}=200\ \mathrm{K}$ . After adsorption of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ , the void volume increased from $16.2\%$ to $20.2\%$ . Based on the single-crystal structures, the framework expansion and distortion were explained by expansion of the interchain distances, slipping of neighbouring chains, and tilting of the $\pi\cdot\cdot\cdot\pi$ stacked phenyl rings of $\mathsf{b z a}^{-}$ ligands (Fig. 18). The adsorbed $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecules were tightly confined in the channel and interact with the phenyl rings on the pore surface via phenyl   $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ –quadrupole interaction. Using the same sealed single-crystal, in situ variable-temperature SCXRD analyses revealed that all the crystal structures adopted the triclinic space group $P\bar{1}$ , and the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake gradually decreased with increased temperature as observed in the variable-temperature adsorption isotherms.109
Variable-temperature $^2\mathrm{H}$ NMR spectroscopy for $[\mathrm{Rh_{2}(b z a-d5)_{4}(p y z)}]$ in a constant $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ pressure $\mathrm{(0.1\MPa)}$ showed that temporary rotations of the ligand phenyl rings were responsible for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ diffusion between the isolated cavities.107 Such thermodynamically unstable states can be hardly visualized by crystallography directly. Similar monoclinic to triclinic phase transitions were also observed for $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ , NO, $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ , $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ and $\mathbf{SO}_{2}$ , which exhibited two-step $\left(\mathbf{N}_{2}\right)$ , S-shaped $\mathrm{(CH_{4})}$ , or typical Langmuir (NO and $\dot{\mathbf{O}_{2}}$ ) isotherm.110–112 On the other hand, the $\mathbf{NO}_{2}$ -loaded crystal maintained the original group space $C2/c$ and the adsorbed $\mathbf{NO}_{2}$ molecules existed as ${\bf N}_{2}{\bf O}_{4}$ .110 The phase transition and structural transformation of $[\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{bza})_{4}(\mathbf{Rpyz})]$ can be altered by ligand substitution.107,113 For example, due to the steric hindrance from the alkyl groups, the 1D chain of $\mathrm{[Rh_{2}(b z a)_{4}(e m p y z)]}$ (empyz $\mathit{\Theta}=\mathit{\Theta}2$ -ethyl-3-methylpyrazine) switches from zigzag to straight before and after CO2 adsorption.113
Takamizawa et al. also observed the precise location of $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ molecules in $[\mathrm{Rh_{2}(b z a)_{4}(p y z)}]$ using in-house SCXRD.114 The $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ adsorption isotherm (77 K) for $[\mathrm{Rh}_{2}(\mathrm{bza})_{4}(\mathrm{pyz})]$ showed two obvious steps, indicating similar structure transformation as for other much heavier gases.107,109 A single crystal of $[\mathrm{Rh_{2}(b z a)_{4}(p y z)}]$ was sealed in a glass capillary with $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ at $77~\mathrm{K}$ and 1 atm, and subjected to SCXRD measurement at $90~\mathrm{K},$ which retained the original space group $C2/c$ with a unit-cell volume $\left(3258\mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{3}\right)$ slightly larger than that of the guest-free form at 93 K $\left(3244\mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{3}\right)$ . Two $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ molecules parallel to each other were strongly confined in the small cavity, forming close contacts with the benzoate edges $\left(\mathbf{H\cdot\cdot\cdotO}\right.=2.57\mathrm{-2.91~\mathring{A}}$ . The number of hydrogen molecules observed in this crystal structure was consistent with the first-step uptake in the isotherm measured at $77\mathrm{~K~}$ . It could be imagined that a crystal structure corresponding to the second-step might be similar to those of the heavier gases, but neither a temperature of $77\mathrm{~K~}$ nor much higher $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ pressure can be achieved by common means. An example of $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ -induced framework distortion accompanying crystal-symmetry change was observed by Wright et al. in a 3D PCP $[\mathrm{Sc}_{2}(\mathrm{bdc})_{3}]$ by using synchrotron SCXRD at $80~\mathrm{K.}^{100}$ However, the location of the seriously disordered $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ could not be determined unambiguously. It should be also noted that, due to the very weak scattering factor of protons for X-ray radiation, neutron diffraction using ${\bf D}_{2}$ as the guest are generally necessary to obtain the location of $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ molecules in porous crystals.115
We used the unique framework flexibility and pore surface structure of MAF-2 to store $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ and separate it from $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ , which showed S-shape isotherms near room temperature, suggesting framework distortion and/or guest–guest interaction around the inflection points.104 Compared with normal
Langmuir-type isotherms, the S-shape isotherm is more beneficial for gas storage and separation, because it exhibits not only high uptake at high pressure but also a lower residue at low pressure. Therefore, MAF-2 can store 40 times more $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ than an empty cylinder at the normal working condition (1.0–1.5 atm for safety reasons). Also, MAF-2 exhibited a very high $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}/\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake ratio of 3.7 at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1 atm, although their adsorption enthalpies differed only by $4.0{-}6.7\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ .95 Single-crystal structures of the guest-free, $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ - and $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ - saturated MAF-2 were measured at $^{123\mathrm{~K~}}$ . The unit-cell parameters of the $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2^{-}}$ and $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ -saturated phases are $1.0\%$ and $0.6\%$ smaller than that of guest-free MAF-2, confirming the role of framework flexibility and stronger binding for $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ The $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ molecule can be refined anisotropically without any restraint, giving a very precise $\scriptstyle\mathbf{C-C}$ bond distance of $1.146(9)\mathring{\mathbf{A}}.$ Similar high-quality single-crystal structures were also obtained even at $293~\mathrm{~K~}$ , indicating the strong fixation of $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ . In contrast, soft restraints on the $\scriptstyle\mathbf{C}=\mathbf{O}$ bond lengths of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ were necessary, and the anisotropic refinement could not be applied at higher temperatures. More interestingly, $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ and $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ locate completely differently in MAF-2. The molecular long axis of $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ points to the planar $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{(Rtz)}_{2}$ fragment of the host framework, forming weak $\mathbf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{H}\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{N}$ interactions with the coordinated $\mathbf{N}$ atoms $(\mathbf{C}\cdot\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{N}\ 3.44\$ and $3.47\mathrm{~\AA}$ , and six $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ molecules form a chair-like supramolecular hexamer with weak $\mathbf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{H}\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{C}$ contacts $\left(\mathbf{C}{\cdot}\cdot\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{C}~3.69~\mathring\mathbf{A}\right)$ (Fig. 19). On the other hand, $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ lies on the $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{Rtz})_{2}$ planar fragment, forming close contact with the coordinated N atoms $\mathbf{(C\cdot\cdot\cdotN\3.40}$ and 3.41 Å) and surrounded by many ethyl groups, and six $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecules form a distorted octahedral arrangement with relatively long intermolecular contacts $(\mathbf{C}\cdot\cdot\cdot\mathbf{O}4.58{\overset{\circ}{\mathrm{A}}})$ . A series of single-crystal structures with different $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ and $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ loadings were also measured at $195\mathrm{~K~}$ , which showed that the framework distortion starts at very low loading and almost finished at $20\%$ loading, and the thermal motion of the host framework decreases with increased loading, being consistent with the energetic trends observed in the gas isotherms. These results demonstrated that in large pores, $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ and $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ with reversed quadruple moments can find their right way to interact with the polar pore surface, even with coordinated azolate N atoms as the most electronegative points. Also, very small framework distortions can effectively change the isotherm shape and relative storage/separation properties.
Using uncoordinated azolate N-donors, $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ can be strongly fixed in the crystal through supramolecular interactions. Recently, we reported a flexible PCP $\mathrm{[Zn_{2}(b t m)_{2}]{\cdot4H_{2}O}}$ (MAF$23{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{b}\mathbf{tm}=$ bis(5-methyl-1H-1,2,4-triazol-3-yl)methane) functionalized with pairs of uncoordinated triazolate N-donors serving as guest-chelating claws.116 MAF-23 showed very high $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption enthalpy of $47.4\pm1.3\mathrm{\kJ\mol^{-1}}$ at near-zero coverage and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ selectivity of 82 at $298\ \mathrm{K},$ as well as an unexpectedly high saturation $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake (3.0 and $2.0{\mathrm{~CO}}_{2}$ per formula unit for observed and predicted values, respectively). Single-crystal structures of MAF-23 $x{\mathrm{CO}}_{2}$ ( $x=0.00$ , 0.07, 1.5, 2.8) were successfully measured at $195\mathrm{K}$ (Fig. 20). The $x$ values were determined by free refinements. Measurement of the singlecrystal structure of $\mathbf{MAF}–23{\cdot}3\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ failed because the crystals saturated with $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ cracked easily, indicating that the crystals expanded enormously at the saturated adsorption state. At near zero loading, i.e., MAF-23 $\cdot0.07\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , a $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecule was chelated by one of the two crystallographically independent guestchelating claws, which possessed a methyl group at the ortho position of the N-donor, demonstrating the weak electron donating effect of the methyl group. Further, there were very short intermolecular $\mathbf{N}{\cdot}\cdot{\cdot}\mathbf{C}$ separations $(2.90(4)–3.00(4)\ \mathring{\mathrm{~A}})$ , being consistent with the high near-zero-coverage $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption enthalpy derived from the sorption isotherms. When the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake increased, the unit-cell volume of MAF-23 continuously increases due to the distortion of coordination geometries of the $\mathrm{Zn(n)}$ ions and ligand shapes around the methylene groups, which explained the excess $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake. Also, two crystallographically different molecular claws in MAF-23 deform in opposite directions with increased $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake, which were interpreted by the convergent attraction and steric repulsion of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecules. At moderate and high loadings, the chelated $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecules in MAF- $23{\cdot}1.5{\bf C}{\bf O}_{2}$ and M $\mathbf{IAF}–23{\cdot}2.8\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ can be anisotropically refined without any restriction even at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ , indicating the exceptionally strong binding affinity of the guest-chelating claws.
Gas adsorption isotherms showing gate-opening or multistep features are generally induced by significant structural transformation of the host framework. For example, Kitagawa et al. observed $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ -induced cleavage and generation of coordination bonds in a flexible PCP $[\mathrm{Cu(pydc)(bpp)}]$ $\mathrm{\bf~\cdot~}\mathrm{\bf~H}_{2}\mathrm{pydc}=\mathrm{\bf~\cdot~}$ pyridine-2,3-dicarboxylic acid; bpp $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}_{1,3}$ -bis(4-pyridyl)propane) possessing a 2D bilayer structure.117 The $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ isotherm at $195\mathrm{~K~}$ showed a gate-opening increase at $P/P_{0}=0.23$ , which was confirmed by SCXRD at the same temperature. Single-crystal structures of guest-free and $\mathbf{CO}_{2}.$ 一 saturated $\mathrm{[Cu(pydc)(bpp)]}$ were measured at 373 and $193\mathrm{~K~}$ , which adopted the space groups $P c a2_{1}$ and Pbcn, and unit-cell volumes of 3843(2) and $4312(5)\mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{3}$ , respectively. The $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption induced the formation of a Cu–pyridyl bond (Cu–N changed from 10.38 to $2.28{\mathring{\mathrm{~A}}})$ , which doubled the numbers of pillars between the two layers, and expanded the thickness of the bilayer from 11.9 to $14.0\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ . There were two independent $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecules, one of which located between two adjacent bilayers while another located inside the internal channel of the bilayer that was originally occupied by the pendant pyridyl groups. Surprisingly, the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecules interact with the carboxylate O atom $\left(\mathbf{C}{\cdot}\cdot\cdot\mathbf{O}\ 2.90{-}3.05\ \mathring\mathrm{A}\right)$ and edges of phenyl rings $\left(\mathbf{O}{\cdot}~{\cdot}\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{C}~3.36{-3.49}~\mathring\mathrm{A}\right)$ on the pore surface, rather than the uncoordinated pyridyl nitrogen atoms or the unsaturated Cu(II) ions (presented in the guest-free state). One of the two independent $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecules was refined anisotropically, indicating strong host– guest interactions. Detailed analysis of the crystal structure shows that this $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ molecule is chelated by a pair of uncoordinated carboxylate oxygen atoms from two carboxylate groups $\left(\mathbf{C}{\cdot}\cdot\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{O}\ 2.90{-}3.04\ \mathrm{\mathring{A}}\right)$ . One of these two uncoordinated oxygen atoms $\left(\mathbf{Cu\cdot\cdotO}2.92\mathring\mathbf{A}\right)$ was displaced from its weakly ligated Cu(II) ion in the guest-free phase $\left(\mathbf{Cu-O}\ 2.63\ \mathring\mathrm{\A}\right)$ . In contrast, another $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ interacts only with a coordinated carboxylate oxygen atom $\left(\mathbf{C}{\cdot}\cdot\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{O}\ 3.05\ \mathring\mathrm{A}\right)$ (Fig. 21). Actually, this $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ -induced structural transformation was first observed during activation of the as-synthesized compound $[\mathrm{Cu(pydc)(bpp)}]{\cdot}5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},$ meaning that $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ gas can play the same role as high boiling-point liquid $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ .
Solvent-induced framework distortion also inspired the elucidation of abnormal ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption behaviour for SOD$[\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{mim})_{2}]$ (MAF-4,118,119 also known as ZIF-8,120 Hmim $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ 2-methylimidazole), which is very thermally and chemically stable and is considered to be a very rigid framework. Twostep ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms are generally observed for MAF-4, which was previously explained as guest rearrangement.120 Nevertheless, the pore volume calculated from its crystal structure $\left(0.54\mathrm{~cm}^{3}\mathrm{~g}^{-1}\right)$ , using the SOLV routine in Platon,121 is consistent with the first-step pore volume $(0.55\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ rather than the second-step $\left(0.67\ \mathrm{~cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{~g}^{-1}\right)$ of the ${\bf N}_{2}$ isotherms.
Moggach et al. found that MAF-4 can distort to open the 4-membered ring apertures at extremely high pressure using a methanol–ethanol mixture as a hydrostatic medium, although the solvent molecules were completely disordered.122 Later, FairenJimenez et al. successfully used the high-pressure structure of MAF-4 to explain the two-step ${\bf N}_{2}$ isotherms by computer simulation and further confirmed by using synchrotron PXRD.123 The $\ensuremath{\mathbf{N}}_{2}$ molecules in the synchrotron PXRD structure of ${\bf N}_{2}@{\bf M A F}{\cdot}4$ were highly disordered. For example, in the centrosymmetric 4-membered ring channel which can only adopt one $\ensuremath{\mathbf{N}}_{2}$ molecule, there were two disordered ${\bf N}_{2}$ molecules resided at the two ends.
We measured a series of variable-temperature single-crystal structures for MAF-4 under open nitrogen flow by using an in-house X-ray diffractometer (Fig. 22).124 The expected phase transition was observed at $113{-}108\mathrm{~K~}$ , below which the imidazolate ligands tilted to open the 4-membered ring. Importantly, the ${\bf N}_{2}$ molecules in the low-temperature phase were highly ordered. Especially, inside the small 4-membered ring channel, an $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ molecule located exactly at the centre. The centrosymmetric nature of this $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ molecule was confirmed by in situ variable-temperature Raman spectra under identical experimental conditions. Below the phase transition temperature, the characteristic Raman peak of ${\bf N}_{2}$ increased significantly, although the ${\bf N}_{2}$ inside the 4-membered ring aperture only constitutes a small portion of the total adsorption amount. An intermediate phase with partially opened 4-membered rings was observed at $111\ \mathrm{K},$ , which was explained as a solid-solution structure of the fully closed and fully opened phases. Similar phase transition and ${\bf N}_{2}$ locations were also observed for the isostructural SOD $\left.\mathsf{I}\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{mtz})_{2}\right]$ (MAF-7, Hmtz $\mathit{\Theta}=\mathit{\Theta}3$ -methyl-1,2,4- triazole) with more polar pore surface and higher guest affinity, which showed a phase transition at $117{-}115\ \mathrm{K}.$ , being ca. $4{-}7\textbf{K}$ higher than that of MAF-4. Due to the sharp phase transition, the intermediate phase of $\mathbf{N}_{2}@\mathbf{MAF}{\cdot}7$ was obtained by a kinetic SCXRD method (time-dependent) instead of the thermodynamic one (temperature-dependent) as used for ${\bf N}_{2}@{\bf M A F}–4$ . These results demonstrated the notable precision and versatility of SCXRD.
A much larger gas-induced framework breathing was observed by Omary et al., who noticed that the two-step ${\bf N}_{2}$ and $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms for $\left[\mathrm{Ag}_{6}(\mathrm{ftz})_{6}\right]$ (FMOF-1, Hftz $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ 3,5-bis(trifluoromethyl)-1,2,4-triazole) implied two pore-filling sequences.125,126 A series of single-crystal structures of FMOF-1 exposed in ${\bf N}_{2}$ at atmosphere pressure were determined at $295\substack{-90\mathrm{~K~}}$ . The temperature-dependent unit-cell parameter profiles exhibited two sections, being coincident with the two-step ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption isotherm. During temperature decrease, the unitcell volume first gradually decreased from 7462 $\mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{3}$ at $295\mathrm{~K~}$ to $6824\mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{3}$ at $119\mathrm{K}$ , and then suddenly increased to $7719\mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{3}$ at $90\mathrm{K}$ , corresponding to a large framework expansion. Six adsorption sites were assigned based on the appearance sequence during temperature decrease. ${\bf N}_{2}$ molecules appeared sequentially from 150 to $119\mathrm{~K~}_{:}$ , at the first three adsorption sites at different positions of the channel, which attracted the channel surface to shrink the crystal. When the measurement temperature further decreased below 119 K, ${\bf N}_{2}$ molecules appeared at the other three adsorption sites inside the originally very small cavities, which expand the cavities and channels, as well as the whole crystal due to steric hindrance effects (Fig. 23). Considering that the pore sizes in the expanded phase are still extremely small, solvent molecules with much larger molecular sizes may not be able to enter these pores, at mild conditions, to induce such a large framework expansion.122
Obviously, depending on the framework flexibility of the PCP and the change of species/amount of the guest molecules, the magnitude of guest-induced structural transformation can vary significantly. Although small structural transformations have been less focused, SCXRD has revealed interesting host– guest and guest–guest structures in such systems. It should be noted that, to precisely compare the crystal structures with different guest-loadings, they should be ideally measured at the same temperature to avoid the thermal expansion effect (see Section 5).
## 4. Structural transformation induced by post-synthetic modification
Although PCPs are less robust than conventional adsorbents, many of them can undergo post-synthetic modification (PSM) by means of covalent reaction on the organic ligands, metal/ ligand exchange, capture and release, and many other means.127–131 The similarity of PXRD patterns between the original and modified samples is usually used as an evidence for retention of framework connectivity, because most PSM examples produce isostructural compounds. Compared to adsorption–desorption of guest molecules, the harsh action of PSM generally produces more damage on the sample crystallinity and single-crystallinity. So far, some SCXRD data have been reported for PCPs after PSM, which not only indicate the robustness of the crystal and exclude recrystallization during solution-based reaction, but also reveal some structural detail of the modified materials. On the other hand, because the changed components in the PCP crystals are usually disordered and/or non-periodic, even SCXRD can hardly determine the modification degree (usually much lower than $100\%$ ) accurately.
## 4.1 Covalent modifications
Solid-state photochemical $\left[2\substack{+2}\right]$ cycloaddition within crystals is a unique solvent-free way to synthesize regio- and stereospecific, highly strained cyclobutane derivatives. Owing to the absence of guest diffusion, the sample single-crystallinity can be usually retained,132–137 but porosity has been rarely demonstrated for these compounds.137 PCP crystals can be covalently modified by condensation of reactive moieties on the pore surface with guest reactants, decomposition of thermal/light sensitive functional groups, and other types of organic reactions.127 While most of these reactions and products are characterized by NMR, mass spectrometry and PXRD, some single-crystal structures of the modified PCP crystals have been reported.138–145 However, most SCXRD data suffer from symmetry-induced disorder, thermal motion of side groups in the large cavities, relatively low conversion degree, and/or degraded single-crystallinity. Only in a couple of examples, SCXRD revealed the structures of the obtained functional group and/or the reaction progress.145,146
Kitagawa et al. utilized an photolysis reaction to produce highly reactive aryl nitrene in a flexible PCP $[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{aipa})_{2}(\mathrm{bpy})_{2}]{\cdot}1{.}5\mathrm{DMF}$ $\mathrm{\bf{H}}_{2}\mathrm{\bf{aipa}}=5$ -azidoisophthalic acid, $\mathbf{CID-N}_{3}.$ ).145 In situ synchrotron SCXRD measurements under an ultrahigh-pressure $\mathrm{Hg}$ lamp were carried out. During the photoirradiation process, the space group of the crystal was gradually transformed from $P2/n$ to $C2/m$ . Comparison of the crystal structures obtained before and after photoirradiation demonstrated the photolysis of the azide groups and generation of nitrene groups, although the latter showed disorder in three positions (Fig. 24). The unstable nitrene groups in the modified crystal can react with $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ molecules to generate nitro and nitroso groups. Similarly, Cohen et al. introduced hydroxy groups onto the pore surfaces of two isostructural PCPs via photolysis of the nitrobenzyl protecting groups.146 SCXRD showed that the resultant framework remained intact but the protecting group nitrobenzyl disappeared.
## 4.2 Coordination modifications
Some PCP crystals can undergo coordination modification to give isostructural materials that may not be accessible by direct self-assembly and crystallization.147–154 Here, coordination modification refers to exchange and capture/release of metal/ ligand ions on the coordination framework (Fig. 25). Exchange of counter ions within the channels of charged host frameworks is a well-known phenomenon. However, due to the reversibility of coordination bonds, whether the ion exchange processes in PCPs undergo the solvent-assisted SCSC transformation or recrystallization is usually controversial. In recent years, the reactivity of PCP crystals with ionic compounds is emerging as a powerful tool for modification of the host framework structures. Regardless of the reaction mechanism (either SCSC transformation or recrystallization), SCXRD is the key method for characterization of the modified materials.
4.2.1 Exchange of metal/ligand ions. As a part of the host coordination framework, the metal ion is cooperatively coordinated by multiple ligands, for which it is expected to be difficult to undergo ion exchange except that on the defects and/or outer crystal surfaces. Nevertheless, not only the counter ion but also the framework metal ion can be exchanged by other metal ions.155,156 For example, Kim et al. reported that the metal ions in the PCP $\mathrm{Cd_{1.5}(H_{3}O)_{3}[(C d_{4}O)_{3}(h e t t)_{8}]\cdot6H_{2}O}$ $\mathrm{\bf~\tilde{H}}_{3}\mathrm{hett}=$ ethyl substituted truxene tricarboxylic acid) could be completely exchanged by Pb(II) in the SCSC manner, and the process was reversible (Fig. 26).157 SCXRD analyses revealed that the crystal before and after metalnode exchange have similar unit-cell parameters but significantly different coordination bond lengths. During the exchange process, little change in the shape, size, or appearance (except some colouring) were observed for a single crystal from a series of optical microscope images, and the preservation of bulk crystallinity was confirmed by in situ PXRD. Increase of the $\mathrm{{\mathbf{P}b}(\mathrm{{\mathbf{\pi}}I I})}$ occupancy and decrease of the Cd(II) were directly revealed by time-dependent in situ SCXRD; the Pb/Cd ratios in the modified crystals were determined by ICP-atomic emission spectroscopy.
Similar to the metal ion, the organic bridging ligand within the host framework might be exchanged by other similar ligands, which is useful to modify the pore surface and pore size of the crystal. For example, Hupp et al. demonstrated that the linkers in highly stable zeolitic PCPs including MAF- $\cdot4^{158}$ and RHO$[\mathrm{Cd}(\mathrm{eim})_{2}]$ (CdIF-4, Heim $=2$ -ethylimidazole)159 can be substituted by other imidazolate ligands in the SCSC fashion, giving isostructural zeolitic frameworks unavailable by direct synthesis. NMR spectroscopy showed that the ligand exchange process was reversible and the exchange percentages can be up to $85\%$ and $100\%$ , respectively, being coincident with the occupancies of the substituent group of imidazolate ligands obtained by SCXRD. More surprisingly, Rosi et al. exchanged the dicarboxylate ligand in $\mathrm{\big(Me_{2}N H_{2}\big)_{4}[Z n_{8}O_{2}(a d)_{4}(n d c)_{6}]{\cdot}4D M F{\cdot}31H_{2}O}$ (bio-MOF-100, Had $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ adenine, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{ndc}~=~2,6$ -naphthalenedicarboxylic acid) by longer dicarboxylates to give isoreticular single crystals with drastically increased void volumes.160 The SCSC transformation processes were proved by a series of optical images of a single crystal at different immersion times.
External metal ions can also exchange the acidic proton on the coordination framework. For example, Cohen et al. showed that the 2-phenylpyridine groups in some PCP crystals consisting of 2-phenylpyridine- $^{5,4^{\prime}}$ -dicarboxylate ligands can react with [IrI(cyclooctadiene) $\left(\mathrm{OCH}_{3}\right)]_{2}$ or $[\mathrm{Rh}^{\mathrm{I}}(\mathrm{cyclooctadiene})\mathrm{Cl}]_{2}$ to give cyclometalated Ir and Rh complexes.161 SCXRD structural analysis confirmed that the $\mathbf{Ir}\big(\mathrm{I}\big)$ or $\operatorname{Rh}(\operatorname{I})$ were chelated by one pyridine nitrogen atom and one carbanion atom from the same dicarboxylate ligand. ICP-MS showed that the frameworks contained Ir or Rh ranging from $0.6\mathrm{wt\%}$ up to $8.3~\mathrm{\wt\%}$ . Lin et al. showed that the free phenolic hydroxyl groups in single crystals of $[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{dhbtc})(\mathrm{DMF})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})]$ 2EtOH4.3DMF $\boldsymbol{4}\mathrm{H}_{2}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{O}}$ $\big(\mathrm{H}_{6}\mathrm{dhbtc\}=\ \big(R\big){-}2,2^{\prime}{.}$ dihydroxy- $^{1,1^{\prime}}$ -binaphthyl- $^{4,4^{\prime},6,6^{\prime}.}$ 一 tetracarboxylic acid) can react with a toluene solution of $\mathrm{Ti(OiPr)_{4}}$ $\begin{array}{r l}{(\mathbf{HOiPr}}&{{}=}\end{array}$ isopropanol) to give $[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{Hdhbtc})\mathrm{Ti}_{0.5}\cdot$ 一 $\left(\mathrm{OiPr}\right)\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)]$ .162 SCXRD structural analysis of the modified crystal revealed that the $\mathrm{Ti}(\mathrm{m})$ ion was coordinated by two deprotonated binaphthol hydroxyl groups from the two independent coordination networks and two original $\mathrm{oiPr}^{-}$ groups in a normal tetrahedral configuration. As a result, the host framework transformed from 2-fold interpenetrated 3D networks to a non-interpenetrated 3D network (Fig. 27).
Exchanging counter ions by ions with coordination abilities can also achieve coordination modification of PCPs. For example, Schro¨der et al. immersed $\mathrm{\big(H_{2}p p z\big)}[\mathrm{In}_{2}(\mathrm{qptc})_{2}]{\cdot}3.5\mathrm{DMF{\cdot}}5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (NOTT-200, ppz $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ piperazine; $\begin{array}{l l l}{{\mathrm{H_{4}q p t c}}}&{{=}}&{{1,1^{\prime},4^{\prime},1^{\prime\prime},4^{\prime\prime},1^{\prime\prime\prime}.}}\end{array}$ 一 quaterphenyl- $3,5,3^{\prime\prime\prime},5^{\prime\prime\prime}$ -tetracarboxylic acid) in a saturated solution of LiCl in water–acetone $(1:1{\bf v}/{\bf v})$ at room temperature for ten days to give $\mathrm{\small{[Li_{1.5}(H_{3}O)_{0.5}I n_{2}(q p t c)_{2}]}\cdot11H_{2}O}$ (NOTT-201), during which the sample retained single-crystallinity (Fig. 28).163 In NOTT-201, the $\mathrm{Li/In}$ molar ratio was determined by ICP-MS to be 0.75. SCXRD analysis showed that NOTT-201 has the same topology of NOTT-200 and all the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{ppz}^{2+}$ cations have been replaced by $\mathrm{Li(\mathrm{_I)}}$ and protons. The $\mathrm{Li(_I)}$ ion was tetrahedrally coordinated by two $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ molecules and two carboxylate oxygen atoms. After exchange of the large guest $\mathrm{{H_{2}p p z}^{2+}}$ , the pore size was increased from $4.3\mathring{\mathrm{~A~}}$ of NOTT-200 to $8.3\mathring{\mathrm{~A~}}$ of NOTT-201. Activation of NOTT-201 can remove the coordinated and lattice $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ molecules. Although the coordinated environment of the $\mathrm{Li(\mathrm{_I})}$ ions in the activated samples was not directly measured, the isosteric heat of $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ adsorption for activated NOTT-201 is higher than that of activated NOTT-200, indicating the strong interaction between $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ and coordinatively unsaturated Li(I).
4.2.2 Capture/release of metal ions. Besides ion exchange, external metal ion can be also introduced onto the host network of PCP crystals, during which the counter anion also enter the pores. Zaworotko et al. reported a series of PCPs $[\mathrm{CdCl(tmpp)}]\mathrm{-}$ $[\mathbf{M}_{x}\mathbf{Cl}_{y}\mathbf{Cd}_{4}(\mathrm{bpt})_{4}(\mathrm{solvent})_{z}]$ $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{tmpp}^{4+}\ =\right.$ meso-tetrakis(4-N-methylpyridyl)porphine; $\mathbf{H}_{3}\mathbf{bpt}=$ biphenyl- $^{3,4^{\prime}}$ ,5-tricarboxylic acid; $\mathbf{M}=$ $\mathbf{Na}^{+}$ , $\mathbf{B}\mathbf{a}^{2+}$ , $\ensuremath{\mathbf{M}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{n}}^{2+}$ , $\operatorname{Cd}^{2+}$ ; $x=1$ , 0.5, 0.5, $1;y=0,0,0,1;z=3,3,2,3$ ; respectively) obtained by soaking single crystals of $[\mathrm{Cd}(\mathrm{tmpp})\cdot$ (solvent) $\mathbb{J}[\mathrm{Cd}_{4}(\mathrm{bpt})_{4}]$ in MeOH solutions of metal chloride salts.164 SCXRD structural analyses revealed that the introduced ions were coordinated by the carboxylate oxygen atoms of the host framework and solvent molecules. Meanwhile, the introduced $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ replaced the coordinated solvent molecules of the cationic $\operatorname{Cd}(\operatorname{II}).$ –porphyrinate guest (Fig. 29). Interestingly, crystallographic refinement and solution-state UV/Vis spectroscopy showed that there was no exchange to the $\operatorname{Cd}(\operatorname{II})$ of both the anionic host network and the cationic porphyrinate guest.
Neutral coordination frameworks can also capture external metal ions. For example, Hardie et al. reported a self-entangled
3D uninodal 4-connected network [Zn(ipa)(bpbpdc)] 2DMF (bpbpdc $\mathbf{\Psi}=N\mathbf{\Psi},N^{\prime}$ -bis(pyridin-4-yl)- $^{\cdot2,2^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine- ${\cdot}5,5^{\prime}$ -dicarboxamide) consisting of a very long $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bipyridyl-type linker with a $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -bipyridyl backbone.165 Interestingly, the $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -bipyridyl fragments of the long bpbpdc ligands were uncoordinated, and each pair of $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -bipyridyl fragments are placed closely in a cross configuration suitable for metal chelation. Soaking the pale-yellow as-synthesized crystals in a DMF solution of $\mathrm{cuCl}_{2}$ for 3 weeks gave yellow–brown crystals of $[\mathrm{ZnCu}_{1/3}(\mathrm{ipa})\cdot$ 一 (bpbpdc $\mathrm{)]}\mathbf{Cl}_{1/3}{\cdot}[\mathbf{CuCl}_{2}]_{0.3}$ nDMF (2/3 of the free chelating sites were occupied by Cu), whose structure and chemical formula were determined by synchrotron SCXRD and energy-dispersive X-ray (EDX) spectroscopy. In the modified crystal, the Cu ion was tetrahedrally coordinated by two $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -bipyridyl moieties from two independent ligands with $\mathrm{{Cu-N}}$ distance $2.066(5)\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ indicating a $+1$ oxidation state (Fig. 30). Obviously, this structural transformation was facilitated by the strong $\mathrm{{Cu}(\Omega)}$ affinity of a pair of crossplaced $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -bipyridyl fragments. In this redox process, the solvent DMF was assigned as the reductant. Due to the additional tetrahedral $\mathrm{{Cu}(\Omega)}$ nodes, the coordination network was partially converted into a binodal 4-connected network.
Besides capture of metal ion or metal salt, the host framework can also release its ionic metal/ligand by extraction. For example, Long et al. showed that the embedded chloride anion in a PCP consisting of $\mathrm{{Cu_{4}}(\upmu_{4}\mathrm{{-Cl})(\up R t a)_{8}}}$ $\left(\mathtt{R t a}^{-}=\mathtt{\Omega}\right.$ tetrazolate or 1,2,3-triazolate group) clusters can be partially extracted along with extraframework $\mathrm{{Cu}(\Omega_{\mathrm{{II}}})}$ cations by immersing the crystal in hot $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}$ for five days.166 SCXRD structural analysis revealed that the $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ at the central position within the $\mathrm{{Cu_{4}}(\upmu_{4}\mathrm{{-Cl})(\up R t a)_{8}}}$ unit was reduced ca. $50\%$ and the lengths of the Cu–N bonds and trans Cu Cu distances were shortened from 2.036(3) and $5.078\mathbf{(}2)\mathring{\mathbf{A}}$ to 1.985(3) and $4.932\acute{(2)}\mathring{\mathbf{A}},$ respectively.
## 5. Structural transformations induced by physical stimuli
Chemical changes of the crystals, such as adsorption– desorption of guest molecules (Sections 2 and 3) and PSM (Section 4), are the commonly encountered origins for structural transformations of coordination polymers. Physical stimuli can also change the structures of coordination polymers without any alternation of their chemical compositions. Unfortunately, many of these physical stimuli-induced structural transformations only occur transiently and can be hardly studied by crystallography. Nevertheless, SCXRD has played an important role in several related fields, such as thermal expansion and spin crossover (SCO), which show small structural alternations among different thermodynamically stable states. Similar to other properties, the host–guest chemistry of PCPs offers an additional parameter to control their framework flexibility and structural responses toward physical stimuli. To distinguish PCPs from other materials, we only discuss examples that are controlled by inclusion of different guest molecules.
## 5.1 Guest-controlled thermal expansion
While thermal expansion behaviours of crystalline materials were usually studied by PXRD,167–171 SCXRD have demonstrated advantages in observing very small structural alterations responsible for thermal expansion in coordination polymers.172–174 For example, Kepert et al. found unusual isotropic negative thermal expansion (NTE) for $\left[\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{btc})_{3}\right]$ (HKUST-1, $\mathbf{H}_{3}\mathbf{b}\mathbf{tc}\ =\ 1{,}3{,}5$ -benzenetricarboxylic acid) with $\alpha=$ $-4.1\times10^{-6}~\mathrm{K}^{-1}$ under vacuum at 100–500 K.175 Based on a series of single-crystal structures measured at $100{-}500\mathrm{K}$ in $16\mathrm{K}$ steps, the NTE behaviour was elucidated by local molecular vibrations within the dinuclear $\mathbf{Cu}_{2}(\mathbf{RCOO})_{4}$ paddle-wheel unit and concerted transverse vibrations of the planar linker btc3.
Since thermal expansion is sensitive to the component, connectivity and interaction in the crystal, guest molecules can be used to modify the thermal expansion behaviour of a porous crystal. However, change of guest-loading can generally induce much larger framework distortion than for temperature, especially for flexible PCPs (see Section 2). Guest-loading is not only dependent on the species and pressure of the surrounding atmosphere. Heating a guest-containing crystal in an open condition is a general method to reduce the guest-loading. In other words, it is usually not easy to measure the pure thermal expansion property for a porous crystal in open conditions. For example, variabletemperature unit-cell parameters of FMOF-1 sealed in vacuum were measured by SCXRD, which showed a very large and constant, positive thermal expansion (PTE) with $\alpha=+230\times10^{-6}\mathrm{K}^{-1}$ and NTE with $\alpha=-170\times10^{-6}\mathrm{K}^{-1}$ .126 When the crystal was exposed in the temperature-controlling $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ flow, the unit-cell parameters changed drastically, giving curved apparent thermal expansion profiles with inflection points at $\begin{array}{r}{119\ \mathrm{K}.}\end{array}$ Since the $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ loading vary at different temperatures, it is not possible to extract the pure thermal expansion effect for the ${\bf N}_{2}$ loaded crystal.
For very rigid and/or large-pore frameworks, variable guestloading may not influence the thermal expansion behaviour of the crystal. When Yaghi et al. used SCXRD to study the adsorption sites for Ar and ${\bf N}_{2}$ within MOF-5, they found NTE behaviour ( $1.2\%$ volume increase from 293 to $30~\mathrm{K}$ , corresponding to $\alpha=-15.3\ \times\ 10^{-6}\ \mathrm{K}^{-1})$ for the crystal sealed with ${\bf N}_{2}$ .96 Later, Snurr et al. elucidated and further predicted the NTE behaviours of isoreticular compounds by molecular simulations,176 and then Iversen et al. investigated the NTE behaviours of MOF-5 under vacuum by SCXRD at $100{-}500\ \mathrm{K}$ and synchrotron PXRD at $80{-}500\ \mathrm{K},$ , which gave $\alpha=-13.1\big(1\big)\times10^{-6}\mathrm{K}^{-1}$ , and the obtained structural details showed that the NTE behaviour was originated from vibrational motions (twisting, rotation and libration) of the bridging ligand.177
To realize true and notable guest-controlled thermal expansion, a flexible PCP should be the first choice. Nevertheless, constant guest-loading must be ensured, which may be fulfilled by strong host–guest interactions (small channels and large/ high-boiling point guests) and suitable measurement conditions (relatively low temperatures and sealed with saturated vapour of the guest to avoid guest escape). To understand the complicated host–guest interplay in the system, not only the small structural alternations of the host framework, but also the detailed host– guest and guest–guest interactions must be visualized.
Using SCXRD, Kepert et al. revealed the guest-dependent NTE behaviours of two nanoporous Prussian Blue analogues $\mathrm{\small{[ZnPt(CN)_{6}]\cdot2H_{2}O}}$ and $\mathrm{\small~\left[CdPt(CN)_{6}\right]}\cdot2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}.$ 178 In situ variabletemperature SCXRD measurements were carried out for direct comparison of the thermal expansion of the as-synthesized and guest-free crystals. While $\mathrm{[ZnPt(CN)_{6}]{\cdot}2H_{2}O}$ $(\alpha=+1.82(15)\ \times\$ $10^{-6}~\mathrm{K}^{-1})$ and $\mathrm{\small{\left[ZnPt(CN)_{6}\right](\alpha=-3.38(9)\times10^{-6}K^{-1})}}$ exhibited PTE and NTE behaviours, respectively, both $\mathrm{\small{[CdPt(CN)_{6}]\cdot2H_{2}O}}$ $(\alpha=-7.31(5)\times10^{-6}~\mathrm{K}^{-1})$ and $\mathrm{[CdPt(CN)_{6}]}$ $(\alpha=-6.69(5)\ \times$ $10^{-6}~\mathrm{K}^{-1})$ showed similar NTE behaviours, which were elucidated by their different cavity sizes (Fig. 31a). The spherical cavities in $[\mathrm{ZnPt}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]$ and $\mathrm{[CdPt(CN)_{6}]}$ were estimated to be ca. 45 and $65\mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{3}$ , respectively. For comparison, an $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ molecule occupies ca. 40 $\mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{3}$ .179 Although the $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ molecules were disordered in the highly symmetric (cubic) single-crystal structures, it can be reasonable to deduce that $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ forms strong interactions with the cyanide ligands on the pore surface of $\mathrm{[ZnPt(CN)_{6}]}_{:}$ which impedes the vibrational motion of the cyanide linkages responsible for the NTE property. For the larger-pore framework of $[\mathrm{CdPt}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]$ , such kind of host–guest interaction is much weaker or even absent, so that its NTE property is essentially independent of $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ inclusion.
Kepert et al. reported another cubic porous framework $[\mathrm{Cd}(\mathrm{CN})_{2}]$ showing similar guest-perturbed NTE properties.180 Crystals of $\operatorname{\left[Cd(CN)_{2}\right]}.\mathbf{CCl_{4}}$ coated with a thin film of grease were subjected for SCXRD measurements, in which the temperatures were controlled by a nitrogen stream. The guest $\mathrm{ccl}_{4}$ escaped above $300\mathrm{~K~}$ to give desolvated crystals $\mathrm{[Cd(CN)_{2}]}{\cdot}x\mathrm{CCl_{4}}$ , in which the $x$ values were determined by SCXRD at $300~\mathrm{~K~}$ At $170{-}375\ \mathrm{~K~}$ the vacant crystal $[\mathrm{Cd}(\mathrm{CN})_{2}]$ $(x~=~0)$ showed the largest isotropic constant NTE coefficient $\alpha=-33.5(5)\ \times$ $10^{-6}~\mathrm{K}^{-1}$ reported so far in crystalline materials. Below $170~\mathrm{K}_{\mathrm{\Omega}}$ $\ensuremath{\mathbf{N}}_{2}$ adsorption became un-negligible, causing apparent PTE behaviours. Such ${\bf N}_{2}$ -adsorption induced framework breathing was also observed for $\mathrm{[Cd(CN)_{2}]}{\cdot}x\mathrm{CCl_{4}}$ with $x=0.64$ and 0.75. On the other hand, single crystals of $\mathrm{[Cd(CN)_{2}]}{\cdot}x\mathrm{CCl_{4}}$ with $x=$ 0.64, 0.75 and 1.00 displayed smaller NTE with $\alpha=-16.9(3)\times$ $10^{-6}~\mathrm{K}^{-1}$ and $\alpha=-5.7\mathrm{(3)}\times10^{-6}~\mathrm{K}^{-1}$ , and even PTE with $\alpha=$ $+10.0(2)~\times~10^{-6}~\mathrm{K}^{-1}$ , respectively. Although the $\mathrm{ccl}_{4}$ guest cannot be clearly seen, owing to symmetry-induced disorder, the guest occupancy could control the extent of host–guest interactions and free molecular vibrations responsible for NTE (Fig. 31b).
Besides large and tunable guest-induced framework distortion, we also found interesting guest-controllable thermal expansion behaviours for the highly flexible framework MCF-18 (Fig. 32).64 The variable-temperature unit-cell parameters of MCF-18, MCF-18 MeOH and MCF-18 DMF were measured by SCXRD at $119{-}295\ \mathrm{K}.$ Single-crystal specimens were sealed in glass capillaries under vacuum or with small amounts of corresponding solvents to ensure constant guest loading. While the shrunk framework MCF-18 showed large and constant PTE on the $c$ -axis with $\alpha=81\times10^{-6}\mathrm{K}^{-1}$ , the expanded frameworks MCF-18MeOH and MCF-18DMF displayed huge but temperature-dependent PTE on the $c$ -axes with maximum coefficients $\ensuremath{\alpha}=242\times10^{-6}\ensuremath{\mathrm{K}}^{-1}$ and $437\times10^{-6}~\mathrm{K}^{-1}$ , respectively. The high porosity and flexibility, as well as the unique framework connectivity of the host framework may transform the large and isotropic thermal expansion of liquids to anisotropic or uniaxial thermal expansion on the crystals. Interestingly, when MCF-18 MeOH and MCF-18 DMF were directly put into the cold ${\bf N}_{2}$ flow at $119\mathrm{K}$ , they possessed much smaller unit-cell volumes than those slowly cooled down from room temperature.
By heating, their thermal expansion profiles gradually approached those of the slowly cooled ones. To the best of our knowledge, this ‘‘quenching’’ effect is similar to that from liquid water to amorphous ice, but has not been observed in other crystalline materials. The slowly-cooled and ‘‘quenched’’ single-crystal structures of MCF-18 DMF were measured at $119\mathrm{~K~}$ and compared with that measured at $295\mathrm{~K~}$ , which revealed some small guest relocations related to temperature change and/or cooling history. However, due to the relatively large channel size and high crystal symmetry (R3%c), the DMF molecules showed serious disorder that required strict restraints, meaning that the guest structures may not reflect their true roles reliably.
Using PCPs with very small and low-symmetry channels, the detailed host–guest interaction may be observed. For example, Barbour et al. reported the solvent-controlled thermal expansion in a microporous framework $\left[\mathrm{Zn(OH)(niba)}\right]$ (Hniba $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ 4-( $1H\cdot$ -naphtho[2,3-d]imidazol-1-yl)benzoic acid), which was a pillared-rod 3D structure consisting of polymeric ${\bf Z}{\bf n}{-}{\bf O}{\bf H-}{\bf Z}{\bf n}$ coordination chains (Fig. 33).181 The guest-free framework exhibited very large PTE $\left(\alpha_{c}=+127\times10^{-6}~\mathrm{K}^{-1}\right)$ and moderate NTE $\left(\alpha_{\alpha/b}\ =\ -21\ \times\ 10^{-6}\ \mathrm{K}^{-1}\right)$ under vacuum at $100{-}370\ \mathrm{~K~}$ SCXRD data of the guest-free crystal were collected at 100, 190, 270 and $370\mathrm{K}_{\mathrm{\Omega}}$ , and structural detail analyses indicated that the remarkable PTE of the $c$ -axis resulted from the increase of the ${\bf Z}{\bf n}{\bf-O H-Z n}$ angle with increased temperature. Interestingly, the PTE coefficient could be changed from $+76\times10^{-6}\mathrm{K}^{-1}$ to $^{+166}\times10^{-6}~\mathrm{K}^{-1}$ (effective at 100–295 K) by loading MeOH, EtOH, $\scriptstyle n-\mathrm{PrOH}$ and i-PrOH. The order of PTE coefficients $\left(\mathrm{MeOH}>\mathrm{EtOH}>n\mathrm{-PrOH}\right)$ can be correlated with the sizes of the guests. Single-crystal structures of the solvated crystals were measured at $100\mathrm{~K~}$ , which revealed that the guest molecules were located around the Zn–OH–Zn coordination chains, forming hydrogen-bonds with the adjacent uncoordinated carboxylate oxygen atoms. The smaller guests (MeOH, EtOH and $\scriptstyle n-\mathrm{PrOH}$ ) gave smaller ${\bf Z}{\bf n}{-}{\bf O}{\bf H-}{\bf Z}{\bf n}$ angles compared with that of the guest-free framework, making the host framework into more ‘‘contracted’’ states to enhance further thermal expansion. In the case of $\mathbf{i}{\mathrm{-PrOH}}$ inclusion, the ${\bf Z}{\bf n}{\bf-O H-Z n}$ angle was larger than that of the guest-free framework, indicating that the host framework was in an ‘‘expanded’’ state restricting the thermal expansion.
Recently, we realized well-defined guest-controlled thermal expansion behaviour in a flexible ultramicroporous framework $[\mathbf{Mn}(34\ –\mathbf{pba})_{2}]$ (MCF-34, 34-Hpba $\mathbf{\lambda}=\mathbf{\lambda}_{3}$ -(pyridin-4-yl)benzoic acid).182 The ligand 34-Hpba with a low energy barrier for conformation change may undergo paddle-wheel movement under external stimuli. The vacant framework possessing ultramicroporous channels can exclude air adsorption $\left(\mathbf{N}_{2}/\mathbf{O}_{2}\right)$ even at $77~\mathrm{~K~}$ In addition, it is stable up to 673 K. Variabletemperature unit-cell parameters of MCF-34 were measured by SCXRD and PXRD, which exhibits constant and huge PTE $\left(\alpha=+224\times10^{-6}~\mathrm{K}^{-1}\right)$ and NTE $\left(\alpha=-107\times10^{-6}~\mathrm{K}^{-1}\right)$ over a wide temperature range (127–673 K) both in vacuum and in open-air condition (Fig. 34). Single-crystal structures of MCF-34 exposed in the temperature-controlling $\ensuremath{\mathbf{N}}_{2}$ flow were measured at 127, 294 and $445\mathrm{~K~}$ , which confirmed the remarkable thermal stability and absence of gas adsorption at low temperature.
Detailed comparison of the crystal structures indicated that the large thermal expansion is attributed to the flexible and porous framework structure. The thermal expansion properties of guest-included frameworks were studied with single crystals in contact with saturated solvent vapour sealed in a glass capillary. While other solvents gave similar thermal expansion behaviours as that of the vacant crystal, the DMF-loaded framework MCF-34 DMF underwent a phase transition at $208\substack{-215\mathrm{~K~}}$ , accompanying a drastic crystal deformation. The thermal expansion coefficients of MCF-34 DMF above $233\mathrm{~K~}$ and below $208\mathrm{~K~}$ were larger and smaller than those of the vacant crystal, respectively, indicating the different aggregation and/or motion of guest molecules. With multiple SCXRD data collected at 127–294 K, the DMF guests were found to be highly disordered in the high-temperature phase, indicating a liquid-like structure and/or symmetry-induced disorder. On the other hand, the low-temperature phase crystal possessed a lower-symmetry space group with a doubled unit-cell volume, in which the location of DMF was well resolved, indicating that the motion of DMF molecules decreased significantly to form a solid-like structure. In the low-temperature crystal structure, a DMF molecule embedded one of its methyl groups into the pore wall, driving the conformation reversion of one-fourth ligands to generate a small pocket. This conformation reversion was transmitted to the whole crystal for a drastic deformation. Nevertheless, crystallography is not able to distinguish whether the DMF molecules are statistically disordered or dynamically moving in the high-temperature phase. Therefore, this liquidlike to solid-like rearrangement of DMF guest was further confirmed by molecular dynamic simulations and solid-state $^2\mathrm{H}$ NMR.
## 5.2 Guest-controlled spin crossover
Tuning the magnetic behaviours of PCPs by guest molecules is a fascinating issue. Compared with other magnetic properties, SCO often occurs at relatively high temperatures, accompanying obvious variations of coordination configuration (usually the coordination bond length), which are generally studied by SCXRD. For examples, Kepert et al. reported guest-controlled SCO behaviours in a series of nanoporous frameworks $[{\mathrm{Fe}}({\mathrm{NCS}})_{2}{\cdot}$ 一 $(\mathrm{bpys})_{2}]$ guest (bpys $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ bipyridyl-type ligands) with diagonally 2-fold interpenetrated rhombic-grid structures and 1D channels.183–187 Variable-temperature SCXRD measurements not only confirmed the significant length changes of $\mathrm{Fe(II){-}N}$ coordination bonds at respective temperatures, but also revealed that the guest molecules can influence the spin states of $\mathrm{Fe(n)}$ ions by forming hydrogen-bonding interactions with the dipyridyl ligands, thiocyanate, and/or changing intraframework and extraframework supramolecular interactions.
The porous pillared-Hofmann-type frameworks $\mathrm{\left[FeM(CN)_{4}(p z)\right]}$ $\mathbf{\left(M=Ni\right.}$ , Pt; $\mathtt{p z}=$ pyrazine) with tetragonal structure is another notable system for guest-controlled SCO behaviours. For example, Kepert et al. showed that both the SCO transition temperatures and hysteresis widths of $\mathrm{\small{[FeNi(CN)_{4}(p z)]}}$ can be tuned by $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ inclusion.188 Compared with the hydrated crystal $\mathrm{\big[FeNi(CN)_{4}(p z)\big]}$ $2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , the guest-free crystal $\mathrm{\big[FeNi(CN)_{4}(p z)\big]}$ has higher SCO transition temperature and wider hysteresis. A hydrated crystal mounted in an open-ended capillary was used for in situ dehydration and SCXRD study. $\mathrm{\small{[FeNi(CN)_{4}(p z)]\cdot2H_{2}O}}$ and $\mathrm{\left[FeNi(CN)_{4}(p z)\right]}$ adopted the orthorhombic space group Pmmm and the tetragonal space group $P4/m m m$ , respectively (Fig. 35). The change of crystal symmetry was attributed to the rotation of the pyrazine pillars in the more open space induced by the removal of lattice $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ guest. The $\mathrm{Fe(n)-N}$ bond lengths of $\mathrm{\small{[FeNi(CN)_{4}(p z)]\cdot2H_{2}O}}$ at $300~\mathrm{~K~}$ (2.128(4)–2.226(6) Å) and $150\mathrm{~K~}(1.946(5)–1.971(5)\mathrm{~\AA~}$ were consistent with the high-spin (HS) and low-spin (LS) states of $\mathrm{Fe(n)},$ respectively. Due to the very short bridging ligands, the unit-cell volume of $\mathrm{\small{[FeNi(CN)_{4}(p z)]\cdot2H_{2}O}}$ increased $15\%$ from LS to HS. Although the $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ molecules were disordered in $[\mathrm{FeNi(CN)_{4}(p z)}]\mathrm{.}$ $2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , the observed relatively short $\mathbf{OH}_{2}{\cdot\cdot\cdot}\pi(\mathbf{pz})$ contacts $\left(3.56\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}}\right)$ could be still indicative, which not only reduced the electron density of the pz ligands but also expanded the host framework to destabilize the LS state and decrease the SCO transition temperature. It should be noted that the orthorhombic phase exhibits pseudo-merohedral twining, which is common for structures with slightly distorted tetragonal topologies. During SCXRD analysis, the orthorhombic structure might be wrongly treated as a tetragonal structure. Nevertheless, PXRD showed that $[\mathrm{FeNi(CN)_{4}(p z)}]\mathrm{.}$ 2H2O possessed a disordered tetragonal P4/m structure,189 which was not consistent with SCO behaviours.188
Tong et al. successfully used an active guest to modify the SCO property of another porous pillared-Hofmann-type framework $\mathrm{\big[FePt(CN)_{4}(p z)\big].}$ 190 The vacant crystal $\mathrm{[FePt(CN)_{4}(p z)]}$ exhibited one-step SCO from fully HS and fully LS with a large hysteresis near room temperature. On the other hand, $[\mathrm{FePt(CN)_{4}(p z)}].$ $0.9\mathrm{C}_{4}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $\left({\bf C}_{4}{\bf H}_{2}{\bf O}_{3}\ =\ \right.$ maleic anhydride) showed two-step SCO from fully HS to $1{:}1\ \mathrm{HS}/\mathrm{LS}$ and further to fully LS with smaller hysteresis at lower temperatures, while $[\mathrm{FePt(CN)_{4}(p z)}].$ $0.9\mathrm{C_{4}H_{4}O_{4}{\cdot}H_{2}O}$ 1 $\mathrm{{\calC}_{4}H_{4}O_{4}=}$ maleic acid) showed one-step SCO from fully HS to $1{:}1\ \mathrm{HS}/\mathrm{LS}$ with even smaller hysteresis and lower transition temperature (Fig. 36). $\mathrm{\small{[FePt(CN)_{4}(p z)]\cdot0.9C_{4}H_{2}O_{3}\cdot H_{2}O}}$ and $\mathrm{\small{[FePt(CN)_{4}(p z)]}}$ $0.9\mathrm{C_{4}H_{4}O_{4}{\cdot}H_{2}O}$ can be interconverted to each other by moistening and heating the crystals, respectively. SCXRD showed that $\mathrm{\small{[FePt(CN)_{4}(p z)]\cdot0.9C_{4}H_{2}O_{3}\cdot H_{2}O}}$ and $[\mathrm{FePt(CN)_{4}(p z)}]\mathrm{.}$ $0.9\mathrm{C_{4}H_{4}O_{4}{\cdot}H_{2}O}$ possessed the same tetragonal space group P4/mmm with only one independent $\mathrm{Fe(n)}$ centre at all representative temperatures. The Fe–N bond lengths of the 1 : 1
HS/LS states were in between those for fully HS and LS states. Although variable-temperature infrared spectroscopy indicated local structural rearrangement in $\mathrm{\small{[FePt(CN)_{4}(p z)]\cdot0.9C_{4}H_{2}O_{3}}}$  $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ , no crystallographic evidence for symmetry breaking was observed for the intermediate states. The guest molecules were highly disordered in the crystal structures, so that the reversible transformation between maleic anhydride and maleic acid was monitored by infrared spectroscopy. Nevertheless, from the contour maps of the residual electron density, the molecular planes of maleic anhydride and maleic acid were observed to be parallel to the sheets. The larger guests which are unfavorable for the framework contraction can stabilize the HS states, resulting in lower transition temperatures and incomplete transition behaviours.
We observed well-defined guest-controlled two-step SCO behaviours and crystallographic data in $[\mathrm{Fe}(\mathsf{b d p t})_{2}]$ guest (Hbdpt $\mathit{\Theta}=\mathit{\Theta}3$ -(5-bromo-2-pyridyl)-5-(4-pyridyl)-1,2,4-triazole, guest $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ Null, MeOH or EtOH) consisting of offset packing square grids (Fig. 37). The guest-free crystal $[\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{bdpt})_{2}]$ was obtained by desolvation treatment of the as-synthesized sample $\mathrm{\big[Fe(bdpt)_{2}\big]{\cdot}E t O H}$ or $\big[\mathrm{Fe(bdpt)}_{2}\big]{\cdot}\mathbf{MeOH}$ . All three phases showed two-step SCO behaviours, in which the transition temperatures $\mathrm{([Fe(bdpt)_{2}]{\cdot}E t O H_{\Delta}<}$ $\mathrm{\left[Fe(bdpt)_{2}\right]}.\mathrm{MeOH}<\mathrm{\left[Fe(bdpt)_{2}\right]})$ and hysteresis widths $([\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{bdpt})_{2}]\cdot$ $\mathrm{EtOH}>\left[\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{bdpt})_{2}\right]\cdot\mathrm{MeOH}>\left[\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{bdpt})_{2}\right]\approx0)$ followed the sizes of the guests. SCXRD data of these compounds were collected at representative temperatures. The HS and LS states possessed the monoclinic space group $P2_{1}/n$ , with only a single type of $\mathrm{Fe(n)}$ centres with characteristic Fe–N bond lengths. The space group changed to $P\bar{1}$ in the intermediate $\scriptstyle\left(1:1{\mathrm{~HS/LS}}\right)$ states, giving two types of $\mathrm{Fe(n)}$ centres corresponding to the HS and LS states, respectively. It should be noted that, the lower-symmetry intermediate phases exhibited intrinsic twinning with unit-cell parameters very similar to those of the higher-symmetry, pure HS/LS phases. During SCXRD analysis of the intermediate phases, the diffraction pattern must be carefully handled as $50:50$ twinning components to obtain the correct $P\bar{1}$ structures. Otherwise, the diffraction pattern could be also well processed and solved in the monoclinic space group $P2_{1}/n$ with only one type of mixed-spin Fe(II) centre. The guest molecules were well-resolved in the small cages, although they were 2-fold disordered due to the crystal symmetry. Welldefined host–guest hydrogen-bonding interactions between alcohol and uncoordinated nitrogen atoms of the triazolate ligands were observed. The $\mathbf{O-H\cdot\cdot\cdotN}$ hydrogen-bond lengths were 2.93(2) and 3.05(2) $\mathring\mathbf{A}$ for EtOH and MeOH, respectively, meaning that EtOH formed stronger hydrogen bonds with the host and reduced the electron density of the triazolate ligands, which lowered the transition temperature and increased the hysteresis widths.
## 6. Conclusions
As shown by the examples discussed above, beyond the routine structural characterization of the as-synthesized crystalline materials, SCXRD has demonstrated significant advantages in the monitoring and explanation for structural transformations of PCPs induced by various types of chemical and physical stimuli, especially for those involving complicated host–guest structural information and small yet important structural alternations. Besides the elucidation of the adsorption affinity/ preference of different sites and/or functional groups on the pore surface for gas absorption, SCXRD can also have other interesting applications. For instance, Fujita et al. showed that the intermediates and products of organic reactions and even trace amount of analytes can be embedded in the single crystals of PCPs and directly visualized by SCXRD.191–195 Of course, SCXRD also play similar roles in other single-crystalline materials such as nonporous coordination polymers and organic crystals, which can also have notable flexibilities and reactivities, although they are out of the scope of this review.
Meanwhile, it is usually a great challenge to keep the sample single-crystallinity after the dynamic processes of activation, adsorption and/or reaction. Thanks to the rapid development of instrumental and structural solution techniques, PXRD is and will be more and more frequently utilized in revealing the structural changes of flexible crystalline materials. Nevertheless, SCXRD is still irreplaceable, because it requires much less factitious restraints in the structure refinement. One should also note that crystallography is not as direct as usually considered. Actually, the basis of crystallography in solving chemical/materials challenges is always based on the knowledge about the chemical information or compositions of the crystal and/or the reaction system. Also, crystallography only observes long-range ordered/averaged, thermodynamically static structural information. As discussed in some examples above, crystallography is less useful and even useless for guest molecules incompatible with the crystal symmetries, dynamically disordered guest molecules and framework components, amorphous and poorly crystalline states, non-periodic defects, etc., which are also sometimes important in the structure transformation processes of PCPs. To address more challenging and more interesting questions and phenomena, an integration of crystallography with in situ spectroscopic and other chemical analytical methods should be very important and promising. To date, the dynamic behaviours and involved mechanisms of flexible PCPs are still mostly rationalized by the structural differences between the thermodynamically stable structures of the initial and final states. In view of the rapid progress/changing of technology, X-ray diffraction should be expected to have a better time-resolution for direct observation of the dynamic process or time-dependent evolution of structural changes.
